WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:09.360
Welcome, everyone to EE 263.

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:12.300
I'm Stephen Boyd.

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:14.230
Today, what I'll
do is first, I'm

00:00:14.230 --> 00:00:16.230
going to just talk a
little bit about the course

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:18.780
mechanics and just
maybe a little bit

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:21.240
of a very high level
about what the course is

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:25.110
and how it's going to evolve
over the quarter, that sort

00:00:25.110 --> 00:00:26.418
of thing.

00:00:26.418 --> 00:00:27.960
And then we'll have
sort of-- there's

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:30.790
an intro lecture, which
is not representative.

00:00:30.790 --> 00:00:34.930
So don't get the
wrong idea about it.

00:00:34.930 --> 00:00:38.280
So that's what we're
going to do today.

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:42.630
The sections will start up
next week, not this week.

00:00:42.630 --> 00:00:46.080
So the way it's going to work
for the class is actually it's

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:47.910
going to be pretty
straightforward.

00:00:47.910 --> 00:00:52.920
Pretty much everything is
going to run through Ed.

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:55.590
The course website
that's here is actually

00:00:55.590 --> 00:00:57.000
going to be really static.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:00.750
So usually, this is something
I would be changing three times

00:01:00.750 --> 00:01:01.320
a day.

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:04.650
And my ambition is to
change it maybe once a week

00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:06.677
or maybe even less.

00:01:06.677 --> 00:01:08.260
So this is going to
be kind of static,

00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:11.530
and you'll find stuff on Ed.

00:01:11.530 --> 00:01:14.680
So let me just go over
some of the prerequisites

00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:20.000
and stuff like that
before we jump in here.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:23.350
So first of all, if you
want to contact the staff,

00:01:23.350 --> 00:01:24.850
you could use Ed.

00:01:24.850 --> 00:01:27.280
You can also use as
a staff email address

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:28.450
and that goes to all of it.

00:01:28.450 --> 00:01:31.900
And I mean, unless there's
some very specific reason why

00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:35.950
you don't want to send
it to all of the TAs,

00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:39.280
please don't send email or
something like that to just one

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:40.390
TA or something like that.

00:01:40.390 --> 00:01:44.150
Or even worse, just to me
because I'm not that reliable.

00:01:44.150 --> 00:01:46.150
And when it goes to
the staff address,

00:01:46.150 --> 00:01:47.200
it goes to all the TAs.

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:50.285
And they're reliable,
I'm not right,

00:01:50.285 --> 00:01:52.660
especially if there's some
important thing in the message

00:01:52.660 --> 00:01:53.230
or whatever.

00:01:53.230 --> 00:01:55.180
I mean, I mostly
reliable, and I try.

00:01:55.180 --> 00:01:58.120
But no guarantees there.

00:01:59.988 --> 00:02:02.280
OK, so let me talk a little
bit about the requirements.

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:03.447
It's pretty straightforward.

00:02:03.447 --> 00:02:05.880
I guess there's like
three, basically.

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:08.340
First is that there's
weekly homework.

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:12.360
And those are assigned
typically one week,

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:15.510
and they're due like the next
Friday or something like that.

00:02:15.510 --> 00:02:17.700
We've actually already
assigned the first real one

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:20.370
which is homework 1.

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:22.740
I mean, after today's
lecture, if you go and look

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:24.355
at it, you might
just say, forget it,

00:02:24.355 --> 00:02:25.980
I'm not taking that
class or something,

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:29.020
which would be a completely
reasonable response to it.

00:02:29.020 --> 00:02:30.480
So you might want to hold off.

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:33.330
But this is not a wait
until next Thursday

00:02:33.330 --> 00:02:34.830
kind of a homework either.

00:02:34.830 --> 00:02:37.020
And I'll talk about
that in a minute

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:39.270
because this is actually
going to take some time

00:02:39.270 --> 00:02:40.900
and things like that.

00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:43.200
There's also a homework
0, which is actually not

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:44.190
really a homework.

00:02:44.190 --> 00:02:48.370
It's more just you
say who you are

00:02:48.370 --> 00:02:50.790
or what you're interested in.

00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:52.410
And there's a couple
of other things

00:02:52.410 --> 00:02:56.220
there that I will also
talk about shortly,

00:02:56.220 --> 00:02:58.710
mostly just giving you a
heads up about some stuff

00:02:58.710 --> 00:03:01.830
coming up in the class.

00:03:01.830 --> 00:03:02.640
So let's see.

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:11.160
There's a midterm quiz and it's
like a 75 minute timed thing

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:12.630
done on Gradescope.

00:03:12.630 --> 00:03:14.460
And it's the fourth
week of the class.

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:18.510
And there'll be just a lot of
flexibility in when you take it

00:03:18.510 --> 00:03:20.250
and all that sort of stuff.

00:03:20.250 --> 00:03:23.680
So generally, for
all of these things,

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:25.620
especially for the final
and for the midterm,

00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:30.180
if you've got some really good
reason why you can't take it

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:32.940
in that period, we'll
be happy to have

00:03:32.940 --> 00:03:35.780
you take it a little bit
early or something like that.

00:03:35.780 --> 00:03:38.190
We'll work with you,
I mean, within reason.

00:03:38.190 --> 00:03:40.710
What we can't do is
actually typically have it

00:03:40.710 --> 00:03:42.595
afterwards because
at that point,

00:03:42.595 --> 00:03:44.220
we've already returned
all these things

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:46.500
and all that sort of stuff.

00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:50.680
And I'll say a little bit about
the midterm quiz in a bit.

00:03:50.680 --> 00:03:52.240
And then there's a final exam.

00:03:52.240 --> 00:03:58.710
So the final exam, by
tradition, I believe it may be--

00:03:58.710 --> 00:04:00.450
there's some questions
as to whether it

00:04:00.450 --> 00:04:02.000
violates University property.

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:04.480
But it is now tenured
or grandfathered.

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:08.770
So the final exam is in
the end of the last week

00:04:08.770 --> 00:04:13.270
and it's a 24 hour
take home exam.

00:04:13.270 --> 00:04:17.200
And it's pretty serious.

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:18.399
Maybe you've heard rumors.

00:04:18.399 --> 00:04:21.760
You just ask someone
who's taken it before.

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:23.140
So, yeah.

00:04:23.140 --> 00:04:26.697
I mean, look, I
doubt some of you

00:04:26.697 --> 00:04:28.780
said, oh, this looks
interesting, I think I'll go.

00:04:28.780 --> 00:04:31.630
I mean, just please--

00:04:31.630 --> 00:04:33.130
well, homework 1
will make it clear.

00:04:35.770 --> 00:04:38.230
If you're-- I don't know--
just innocent and said, oh,

00:04:38.230 --> 00:04:40.090
I heard about it, but you
didn't ask friends about it,

00:04:40.090 --> 00:04:41.715
you might want to
ask friends about it.

00:04:41.715 --> 00:04:45.050
It's not the easiest
way to earn three units.

00:04:45.050 --> 00:04:46.900
Let me just put it that way.

00:04:46.900 --> 00:04:48.200
The class.

00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:50.540
But it's super useful.

00:04:50.540 --> 00:04:55.095
So I'm hoping your friends-- it
depends who your friends are.

00:04:55.095 --> 00:04:56.470
If you have good
friends, they'll

00:04:56.470 --> 00:04:58.180
confirm that it's useful.

00:04:58.180 --> 00:04:59.900
I mean, people who
had taken the class.

00:04:59.900 --> 00:05:01.900
If they don't say that,
then you should probably

00:05:01.900 --> 00:05:03.370
change your friends.

00:05:03.370 --> 00:05:05.890
That would be my recommendation.

00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:13.450
And the grading is the
homework is 20%, midterms 15%,

00:05:13.450 --> 00:05:14.200
final exam--

00:05:14.200 --> 00:05:16.600
I mean, we might
change these things.

00:05:16.600 --> 00:05:18.340
I doubt we will.

00:05:18.340 --> 00:05:20.840
So the class is actually
an enormous amount of work.

00:05:20.840 --> 00:05:22.100
But I don't mind--

00:05:22.100 --> 00:05:24.790
I mean, you probably
guess this anyway

00:05:24.790 --> 00:05:27.850
or, actually, you can find out
yourself that the final grade

00:05:27.850 --> 00:05:29.230
distribution is pretty high.

00:05:29.230 --> 00:05:31.540
So what we like to think
about in the class is

00:05:31.540 --> 00:05:34.510
you'll work really
hard for 10 weeks,

00:05:34.510 --> 00:05:37.735
finished with this like
crazy 24 hour take home exam.

00:05:39.718 --> 00:05:42.010
A lot of people think of it
as actually a very positive

00:05:42.010 --> 00:05:45.940
experience because it's not one
of these nonsense regurgitation

00:05:45.940 --> 00:05:52.210
things that ChatGPT could
easily do, easily do.

00:05:52.210 --> 00:05:55.540
It is not one of those.

00:05:55.540 --> 00:05:59.740
Hopefully, you'll finish
the 24 hour take home exam

00:05:59.740 --> 00:06:02.950
thinking, wow, I
actually did some stuff

00:06:02.950 --> 00:06:04.160
in the last 24 hours.

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:06.310
I mean, it'll be on real
applications and things

00:06:06.310 --> 00:06:07.330
like that.

00:06:07.330 --> 00:06:10.630
And so hopefully, you'll
actually be tired,

00:06:10.630 --> 00:06:12.030
but feel good about it.

00:06:12.030 --> 00:06:16.810
Sort of like a nice
rigorous hike or something.

00:06:16.810 --> 00:06:19.420
OK, so that that's
the final exam.

00:06:19.420 --> 00:06:25.330
And by the way, homework
0 has something on that

00:06:25.330 --> 00:06:29.020
because otherwise, if
you have any objection

00:06:29.020 --> 00:06:31.600
to this, to the idea that--

00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:35.050
some people come to me
and they say, oh, my God,

00:06:35.050 --> 00:06:38.357
do you realize what
fraction of my grade

00:06:38.357 --> 00:06:39.940
is concentrated in
this 24 hour period

00:06:39.940 --> 00:06:41.190
and what if I don't feel good.

00:06:41.190 --> 00:06:43.030
And I'm like, OK, whatever.

00:06:43.030 --> 00:06:48.660
So anyway, this is
just full disclosure.

00:06:48.660 --> 00:06:51.780
So yeah, that's
right, that's right.

00:06:51.780 --> 00:06:54.790
Don't take the class
if that's a problem.

00:06:54.790 --> 00:06:57.420
So let's see.

00:06:57.420 --> 00:07:02.730
Now I'll say a little bit
about how the class goes.

00:07:02.730 --> 00:07:05.068
Actually, it's interesting
to talk about the trajectory

00:07:05.068 --> 00:07:05.610
of the class.

00:07:05.610 --> 00:07:06.900
And this is important
just-- and I

00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:09.025
will remind you at several
points during the class.

00:07:09.025 --> 00:07:11.520
So here's the structure
of the class--

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:12.600
today doesn't count.

00:07:12.600 --> 00:07:14.380
It's like an intro
overview thing.

00:07:14.380 --> 00:07:15.240
It's nothing.

00:07:15.240 --> 00:07:17.190
Starting Thursday--
maybe even today.

00:07:17.190 --> 00:07:18.600
Depends on the pacing.

00:07:18.600 --> 00:07:21.180
But today, we might even start
it with the real material.

00:07:21.180 --> 00:07:27.080
For the first three weeks, the
class is basically just math.

00:07:27.080 --> 00:07:28.533
So that's cool.

00:07:28.533 --> 00:07:29.450
I was trained in math.

00:07:29.450 --> 00:07:30.200
I like math.

00:07:30.200 --> 00:07:32.732
But I also am pretty
well socialized.

00:07:32.732 --> 00:07:34.190
My parents were
English professors.

00:07:34.190 --> 00:07:37.590
So I knew at an early age,
you should do that, but never

00:07:37.590 --> 00:07:39.590
speak about it in public
or something like that.

00:07:39.590 --> 00:07:42.020
That's fine if you like math.

00:07:42.020 --> 00:07:44.458
So the point is for I know
that we have a lot of people--

00:07:44.458 --> 00:07:46.250
I mean, we have a lot
of people in the room

00:07:46.250 --> 00:07:49.340
here who have quite
good training in math.

00:07:49.340 --> 00:07:50.180
Good for you.

00:07:50.180 --> 00:07:51.440
That's fantastic.

00:07:51.440 --> 00:07:54.662
And I think it'll be
entertaining for those people.

00:07:54.662 --> 00:07:56.120
I think we have a
lot of people who

00:07:56.120 --> 00:07:57.600
are more interested
in applications

00:07:57.600 --> 00:07:58.970
and they're in a field.

00:07:58.970 --> 00:08:01.580
And they just want
to know what could--

00:08:01.580 --> 00:08:05.000
so to you, the first three
weeks is going to be--

00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:07.700
if you don't look up three
weeks into this class

00:08:07.700 --> 00:08:11.270
and say, what am I
doing, all my friends

00:08:11.270 --> 00:08:13.880
told me to take this class,
it's like this is insane,

00:08:13.880 --> 00:08:16.130
how did I get into this, right?

00:08:16.130 --> 00:08:19.460
If you don't experience that
feeling, then I don't know,

00:08:19.460 --> 00:08:20.930
there's something
weird about you.

00:08:20.930 --> 00:08:21.740
But maybe not.

00:08:21.740 --> 00:08:23.510
I mean, it's fine.

00:08:23.510 --> 00:08:26.010
So all I'm saying is expect
the first three weeks

00:08:26.010 --> 00:08:28.320
to just be a lot of
math, and you're just

00:08:28.320 --> 00:08:32.159
thinking why did I
sign up for this class

00:08:32.159 --> 00:08:34.320
or something like that.

00:08:34.320 --> 00:08:38.370
That's all completely normal.

00:08:38.370 --> 00:08:41.039
And the midterm quiz
kind of caps that.

00:08:41.039 --> 00:08:44.670
So the midterm quiz
is, in fact, it's

00:08:44.670 --> 00:08:48.688
like a very traditional silly
thing like a calculus class

00:08:48.688 --> 00:08:49.230
or something.

00:08:49.230 --> 00:08:54.930
You just have to learn period
like 20 or 30 common convex,

00:08:54.930 --> 00:08:56.640
concave functions,
you need to learn

00:08:56.640 --> 00:09:01.590
the rules about how you combine
them and basic stuff like that.

00:09:01.590 --> 00:09:05.400
And then that's all that we're
going to check in this midterm.

00:09:05.400 --> 00:09:08.490
And that's just basic stuff.

00:09:08.490 --> 00:09:11.100
And we will make no
effort whatsoever

00:09:11.100 --> 00:09:14.480
at making it interesting.

00:09:14.480 --> 00:09:15.480
None.

00:09:15.480 --> 00:09:17.730
I mean, we tried and it
was like as falling flat.

00:09:17.730 --> 00:09:19.050
It just doesn't work.

00:09:19.050 --> 00:09:24.090
But trust us, we're not going
to do any math gratuitously.

00:09:24.090 --> 00:09:25.780
We will use all of it.

00:09:25.780 --> 00:09:31.230
And so if you survive
the first three weeks,

00:09:31.230 --> 00:09:33.518
then it gets fun because
all of a sudden--

00:09:33.518 --> 00:09:35.310
also, you're not going
to get all the stuff

00:09:35.310 --> 00:09:36.360
in the first three weeks.

00:09:36.360 --> 00:09:38.220
For one thing, it's
just weird and abstract

00:09:38.220 --> 00:09:41.610
and you're like, who
would care about this.

00:09:41.610 --> 00:09:44.620
Anyway, the answer is
you four weeks from now.

00:09:44.620 --> 00:09:46.350
So that's the answer.

00:09:46.350 --> 00:09:48.690
And you'll see that-- so
all these things, you're

00:09:48.690 --> 00:09:51.390
like, who care what's the
difference between maximal

00:09:51.390 --> 00:09:53.940
and maximum, I
couldn't care less

00:09:53.940 --> 00:09:56.250
or who cares if the log
determinant-- anyway, what'll

00:09:56.250 --> 00:09:59.310
happen is we'll transition
in the middle of the course

00:09:59.310 --> 00:10:01.260
to doing applications.

00:10:01.260 --> 00:10:03.360
And when you do
applications, then all

00:10:03.360 --> 00:10:05.430
of a sudden, what
happens is a lot

00:10:05.430 --> 00:10:07.470
of people tell me, the
stuff from the beginning

00:10:07.470 --> 00:10:10.012
of the class, which
they kind of got--

00:10:10.012 --> 00:10:11.970
but it's the middle of
the class it's cemented.

00:10:11.970 --> 00:10:13.560
Because once you start
using it, you're like, oh,

00:10:13.560 --> 00:10:15.510
yeah, OK, cool, that's
the entropy, that's

00:10:15.510 --> 00:10:17.640
the log of the volume
of an ellipsoid, that's

00:10:17.640 --> 00:10:18.720
something like that.

00:10:18.720 --> 00:10:21.250
And then it all makes sense.

00:10:21.250 --> 00:10:23.520
So that's the other
thing to not worry about.

00:10:23.520 --> 00:10:25.818
If you have a sense
that, A, you signed up

00:10:25.818 --> 00:10:27.360
for the wrong class
from now, and, B,

00:10:27.360 --> 00:10:30.570
you're only getting 70% of
it, at the three week mark,

00:10:30.570 --> 00:10:31.890
this is normal.

00:10:31.890 --> 00:10:33.125
I'm just saying it.

00:10:33.125 --> 00:10:35.250
Actually, I might repeat
it because a lot of people

00:10:35.250 --> 00:10:36.870
don't actually really--
they're like, oh,

00:10:36.870 --> 00:10:38.162
yeah, that's funny, ha, ha, ha.

00:10:38.162 --> 00:10:40.720
But it's real.

00:10:40.720 --> 00:10:42.270
So we'll see.

00:10:42.270 --> 00:10:43.200
Let's see.

00:10:43.200 --> 00:10:46.170
Oh, I should say
one more thing--

00:10:46.170 --> 00:10:48.850
this year is actually
the first one

00:10:48.850 --> 00:10:52.980
where we are only going to
support Python in the class.

00:10:52.980 --> 00:10:55.860
So you're going to use CVXPY.

00:10:55.860 --> 00:11:01.460
There are other functionally
equivalent things.

00:11:01.460 --> 00:11:07.350
There's Convex.jl in Julia,
there's CVX in Matlab,

00:11:07.350 --> 00:11:14.440
there's CVXR, there's probably
maybe a couple of others.

00:11:14.440 --> 00:11:18.010
So you're welcome to use those.

00:11:18.010 --> 00:11:20.590
Certainly, if you're in
statistics and you use R,

00:11:20.590 --> 00:11:23.680
great, I mean, fire
up CVXR just for fun.

00:11:23.680 --> 00:11:26.140
But what it means
is that post-class,

00:11:26.140 --> 00:11:29.140
you have some stuff you can use.

00:11:29.140 --> 00:11:29.980
That's good.

00:11:29.980 --> 00:11:33.830
But for this class, we're
not going to support it.

00:11:33.830 --> 00:11:38.440
By the way, a lot of
the homework exams--

00:11:38.440 --> 00:11:40.540
sorry-- a lot of the
homework problems

00:11:40.540 --> 00:11:44.320
were old homework
final exam problems.

00:11:44.320 --> 00:11:46.720
And a lot of them
do have support

00:11:46.720 --> 00:11:51.243
for other like for Matlab or
Julia or something like that.

00:11:51.243 --> 00:11:52.660
And if you're
curious and you want

00:11:52.660 --> 00:11:55.760
to shadow it in some other
language, by all means,

00:11:55.760 --> 00:11:56.290
go ahead.

00:11:56.290 --> 00:11:58.990
But just to be completely
clear about it,

00:11:58.990 --> 00:12:02.170
you will be using CVXPY.

00:12:02.170 --> 00:12:04.510
And it depends on
your learning style,

00:12:04.510 --> 00:12:08.230
but it depends on
your learning style.

00:12:08.230 --> 00:12:14.140
But maybe next week, early next
week, you can pip install CVXPY

00:12:14.140 --> 00:12:17.440
or whatever it is,
however it's installed.

00:12:20.020 --> 00:12:23.560
Let me see if there's anything
else I've missed about this.

00:12:23.560 --> 00:12:27.620
Any questions about-- yes.

00:12:27.620 --> 00:12:30.440
Well, I guess it's not maybe
you touched on it a little bit.

00:12:30.440 --> 00:12:33.890
But say, for example,
