00:12:33.890 --> 00:12:37.250
what should I be
thinking about right now?

00:12:37.250 --> 00:12:39.040
What should I
prepare myself for?

00:12:39.040 --> 00:12:42.440
OK, so EE-263 is not actually--

00:12:42.440 --> 00:12:44.190
I didn't say anything
about prerequisites.

00:12:44.190 --> 00:12:45.648
So let me say
something about that.

00:12:45.648 --> 00:12:46.850
Thank you for reminding me.

00:12:46.850 --> 00:12:49.400
So the prerequisites
is actually mostly just

00:12:49.400 --> 00:12:53.240
some mathematical maturity.

00:12:53.240 --> 00:12:55.850
So you don't have to have seen
any applications that we're

00:12:55.850 --> 00:12:59.270
going to cover because
we'll give you all

00:12:59.270 --> 00:13:01.170
the details for all of them.

00:13:01.170 --> 00:13:03.170
Now of course, by the
way, the flip side of that

00:13:03.170 --> 00:13:06.392
is if you're in a field
and we're covering it,

00:13:06.392 --> 00:13:07.850
you're going to be
cringing because

00:13:07.850 --> 00:13:09.892
of all the gross
simplifications in [INAUDIBLE]..

00:13:09.892 --> 00:13:15.200
And by the way, and
you're welcome to either--

00:13:15.200 --> 00:13:17.450
I can usually tell
who's an expert when

00:13:17.450 --> 00:13:19.200
I'm talking about
something because people

00:13:19.200 --> 00:13:21.530
are going like, oh, my God,
oh, no, no, how could he--

00:13:21.530 --> 00:13:24.290
anyway, but you're welcome
to pipe up at that.

00:13:24.290 --> 00:13:25.830
In fact, I may even call on you.

00:13:25.830 --> 00:13:28.560
If I detect that you were
an expert in something

00:13:28.560 --> 00:13:32.250
I am currently butchering,
I will actually

00:13:32.250 --> 00:13:35.280
possibly call on you to make a
comment or something like that.

00:13:37.950 --> 00:13:42.420
So you should know some
linear algebra, probability.

00:13:42.420 --> 00:13:44.430
Those two are like
just non-negotiable.

00:13:44.430 --> 00:13:46.110
We're not going to
sit down and have

00:13:46.110 --> 00:13:49.290
someone say what's a
density or something.

00:13:49.290 --> 00:13:51.600
And that means
these days, if you

00:13:51.600 --> 00:13:53.700
took some kind of nonsense
machine learning class

00:13:53.700 --> 00:13:56.670
where you didn't learn
anything, and you think you now

00:13:56.670 --> 00:14:00.360
know linear algebra and
probability, you're wrong,

00:14:00.360 --> 00:14:03.240
and you will find out.

00:14:03.240 --> 00:14:07.740
But I mean, nothing
wrong with those classes,

00:14:07.740 --> 00:14:09.630
but you take that
class, and you actually

00:14:09.630 --> 00:14:12.180
know a few other things,
and you'll be fine.

00:14:12.180 --> 00:14:15.300
So these are not hard
prerequisites by any means

00:14:15.300 --> 00:14:16.580
at all.

00:14:16.580 --> 00:14:18.660
Trying to think what
else you'd need to know.

00:14:18.660 --> 00:14:21.900
Oh, obviously, you can't
be afraid of firing up

00:14:21.900 --> 00:14:23.940
a Python notebook.

00:14:23.940 --> 00:14:26.090
You will not do
actual programming

00:14:26.090 --> 00:14:28.590
because you're going to write
scripts that are like 10 lines

00:14:28.590 --> 00:14:30.600
long, I mean, basically.

00:14:30.600 --> 00:14:33.550
So to me, that doesn't
cross over the threshold

00:14:33.550 --> 00:14:35.305
to programming.

00:14:35.305 --> 00:14:37.680
But you have to know what a
notebook is and know your way

00:14:37.680 --> 00:14:40.740
a little bit around Python
and object oriented things,

00:14:40.740 --> 00:14:42.090
but that's all.

00:14:42.090 --> 00:14:46.310
So nothing fancy,
nothing advanced.

00:14:46.310 --> 00:14:48.230
OK, let's see what else.

00:14:48.230 --> 00:14:50.300
There's one more thing
I was going to mention,

00:14:50.300 --> 00:14:54.110
and that is-- and you'll
see this on homework 1.

00:14:54.110 --> 00:14:56.630
So the TAs and I,
we've been having

00:14:56.630 --> 00:14:59.750
a lot of fun with ChatGPT.

00:14:59.750 --> 00:15:01.580
I mean, us and a
million other people.

00:15:01.580 --> 00:15:05.630
But anyway, so we have
typed in interesting--

00:15:05.630 --> 00:15:11.300
actually homework problems,
final exam problems.

00:15:11.300 --> 00:15:13.435
And there was actually
one final exam problem.

00:15:13.435 --> 00:15:14.810
It was a very
straightforward one

00:15:14.810 --> 00:15:17.510
where it actually came up with
a completely reasonable solution

00:15:17.510 --> 00:15:20.840
and generated code that ran.

00:15:20.840 --> 00:15:22.610
But that's cool
because that was--

00:15:22.610 --> 00:15:27.830
we have a category of final exam
problem where it just goes like

00:15:27.830 --> 00:15:28.430
this--

00:15:28.430 --> 00:15:30.830
we just tell you
exactly what to do.

00:15:30.830 --> 00:15:34.310
We say, here's a field, you
have to decide these things,

00:15:34.310 --> 00:15:37.080
here's this flux balance
constraint, here's this thing,

00:15:37.080 --> 00:15:39.080
here's another constraint,
here's the objective.

00:15:39.080 --> 00:15:41.420
And then what happens is you
just assemble it, write it

00:15:41.420 --> 00:15:44.270
into six lines of
Python and run it.

00:15:44.270 --> 00:15:46.700
And presumably, there's an
example there that shows you

00:15:46.700 --> 00:15:49.670
you just did something good.

00:15:49.670 --> 00:15:52.038
If it's an image thing,
a Smiley face comes up.

00:15:52.038 --> 00:15:52.580
I don't know.

00:15:52.580 --> 00:15:54.470
That kind of thing.

00:15:54.470 --> 00:15:57.470
So we have those.

00:15:57.470 --> 00:15:59.630
Actually, once I had a
hilarious thing where

00:15:59.630 --> 00:16:00.920
someone turned in his final.

00:16:00.920 --> 00:16:03.330
And I said, how was it.

00:16:03.330 --> 00:16:06.440
And he said, yeah,
I mean, problem 3--

00:16:06.440 --> 00:16:09.620
I was like, problem 3, that was
like we told you exactly-- he

00:16:09.620 --> 00:16:13.340
said, yeah, but I
didn't trust you.

00:16:13.340 --> 00:16:15.713
He was like, I
read it, I did it.

00:16:15.713 --> 00:16:17.630
I said, yeah, but did
the Smiley face come up.

00:16:17.630 --> 00:16:19.220
And he goes, yeah,
it came up, sure.

00:16:19.220 --> 00:16:21.180
And I said, well, that's it,
that was the whole problem,

00:16:21.180 --> 00:16:22.138
there was nothing else.

00:16:22.138 --> 00:16:24.300
And he said like, yeah,
but you're kind of devious,

00:16:24.300 --> 00:16:27.452
and I thought maybe it would be
just like you to put something

00:16:27.452 --> 00:16:29.410
that looks like it was
totally straightforward,

00:16:29.410 --> 00:16:32.310
but actually wasn't.

00:16:32.310 --> 00:16:33.570
Anyway, so just heads up.

00:16:33.570 --> 00:16:35.153
Some of them are
just straightforward.

00:16:35.153 --> 00:16:36.975
Anyway, the rest of the--

00:16:40.528 --> 00:16:41.445
I'll have to fix this.

00:16:49.030 --> 00:16:52.660
However, on the rest, it
produced the most hilarious

00:16:52.660 --> 00:16:53.230
stuff ever.

00:16:53.230 --> 00:16:54.940
It was just completely awesome.

00:16:54.940 --> 00:17:00.250
It was beautifully
written and 100% wrong

00:17:00.250 --> 00:17:01.540
which is actually for me--

00:17:01.540 --> 00:17:04.480
because usually, when you see
things that are totally wrong,

00:17:04.480 --> 00:17:05.750
they're also poorly written.

00:17:05.750 --> 00:17:06.970
So there's a bit of
a correlation there.

00:17:06.970 --> 00:17:08.170
When you see things
beautifully written,

00:17:08.170 --> 00:17:10.550
it's typically someone
knows what they're doing.

00:17:10.550 --> 00:17:14.410
So this was like a
weird combination

00:17:14.410 --> 00:17:18.339
of beautifully written and
utterly and totally wrong

00:17:18.339 --> 00:17:22.359
from beginning to end,
which is cool, but--

00:17:22.359 --> 00:17:27.069
So a couple of us who teach
this class and similar--

00:17:27.069 --> 00:17:31.660
Mert Pilanci-- we want
to actually train a--

00:17:31.660 --> 00:17:33.820
I mean, it can't be
that hard to train

00:17:33.820 --> 00:17:35.920
one of these large
language models

00:17:35.920 --> 00:17:39.010
to do this kind of stuff,
at least the basic stuff

00:17:39.010 --> 00:17:41.830
and be correct.

00:17:41.830 --> 00:17:46.610
I mean, if they can train it to
emit code that executes mostly,

00:17:46.610 --> 00:17:50.450
then they can definitely get
the basics of convex analysis

00:17:50.450 --> 00:17:51.920
right.

00:17:51.920 --> 00:17:57.740
Anyway, so to do that, we want
to actually use the discussion

00:17:57.740 --> 00:18:00.770
forum that we will use for
this quarter at the end

00:18:00.770 --> 00:18:03.260
of the quarter to train
something like that--

00:18:03.260 --> 00:18:04.560
and who knows what will happen.

00:18:04.560 --> 00:18:06.560
But it might be kind of
cool to actually come up

00:18:06.560 --> 00:18:08.780
with something that'd
be like a live thing

00:18:08.780 --> 00:18:10.880
that people would do
and could ask questions

00:18:10.880 --> 00:18:14.600
and we won't need TAs
anymore or something.

00:18:14.600 --> 00:18:16.285
I don't know.

00:18:16.285 --> 00:18:18.410
Although, I have to say,
from the things I've seen,

00:18:18.410 --> 00:18:20.840
we definitely still need TAs.

00:18:20.840 --> 00:18:22.580
So you're safe for
this quarter for sure.

00:18:22.580 --> 00:18:24.890
Don't worry.

00:18:24.890 --> 00:18:26.090
Anyway, so that's the idea.

00:18:26.090 --> 00:18:28.670
And for that, we had some
hilarious conversations

00:18:28.670 --> 00:18:31.850
with the University privacy
office and it ended up

00:18:31.850 --> 00:18:37.440
with us on homework 0, asking
whether you're OK with using

00:18:37.440 --> 00:18:40.140
your chat things which--

00:18:40.140 --> 00:18:43.020
and you're welcome to say no and
you can change your mind later.

00:18:43.020 --> 00:18:46.440
But it would be all
anonymized, no one

00:18:46.440 --> 00:18:48.510
would know anything,
and stuff like that.

00:18:48.510 --> 00:18:50.580
So all cool.

00:18:50.580 --> 00:18:54.090
So I think that might cover
pretty much everything.

00:18:54.090 --> 00:18:56.670
Any questions?

00:18:56.670 --> 00:18:57.650
Yeah.

00:18:57.650 --> 00:19:01.210
Just going to be planting some
incorrect answers in the editor

00:19:01.210 --> 00:19:02.430
discretion, just--

00:19:02.430 --> 00:19:03.480
Yeah, that would be good.

00:19:03.480 --> 00:19:04.360
Thank you very much.

00:19:04.360 --> 00:19:05.360
That would be very good.

00:19:05.360 --> 00:19:07.670
Yeah.

00:19:07.670 --> 00:19:11.460
That would be good, yeah.

00:19:11.460 --> 00:19:14.760
Yeah, it's really entertaining.

00:19:14.760 --> 00:19:17.190
But my feeling is I think
we can get it right.

00:19:17.190 --> 00:19:19.613
So I guess part of
it is the beginning

00:19:19.613 --> 00:19:21.780
of the class is just--
because there's just not that

00:19:21.780 --> 00:19:23.730
many things you need to know.

00:19:23.730 --> 00:19:25.890
And so it's sort
of like learning

00:19:25.890 --> 00:19:28.620
how to decline verbs in
Spanish or something like that.

00:19:28.620 --> 00:19:31.110
I mean, it's mechanical.

00:19:31.110 --> 00:19:35.040
You probably don't
need a live teacher

00:19:35.040 --> 00:19:37.920
to do this and, for that
matter, pronouncing correctly,

00:19:37.920 --> 00:19:40.000
getting your tones
in Chinese correctly.

00:19:40.000 --> 00:19:43.350
That can be automated now too.

00:19:43.350 --> 00:19:45.880
So my feeling is like
in some weird way,

00:19:45.880 --> 00:19:47.640
the first three weeks
of this class, which

00:19:47.640 --> 00:19:52.140
are kind of just those
two things, similar,

00:19:52.140 --> 00:19:54.757
could actually be at some
point like quasi automated

00:19:54.757 --> 00:19:56.590
or at least we'd have
a lot of help doing it

00:19:56.590 --> 00:19:58.007
and stuff like
that, which I think

00:19:58.007 --> 00:20:00.910
it'd be kind of cool because
then we could jump in.

00:20:00.910 --> 00:20:02.030
Yes.

00:20:02.030 --> 00:20:04.202
How much probability
is related to that?

00:20:04.202 --> 00:20:05.410
How much probability-- basic.

00:20:05.410 --> 00:20:07.300
You need to know
probability 101,

00:20:07.300 --> 00:20:10.480
but you really need to know it.

00:20:10.480 --> 00:20:11.470
It's very basic stuff.

00:20:11.470 --> 00:20:12.928
You need to know
what a density is,

00:20:12.928 --> 00:20:15.130
what a CDF is, what
conditioning is,

00:20:15.130 --> 00:20:16.600
what independence really means.

00:20:16.600 --> 00:20:19.870
So it's all probability 101.

00:20:19.870 --> 00:20:23.680
If we go beyond that,
we'll remind you

00:20:23.680 --> 00:20:25.390
what a moment
generating function

00:20:25.390 --> 00:20:26.590
is or something like that.

00:20:26.590 --> 00:20:28.090
So, yeah.

00:20:28.090 --> 00:20:30.960
OK, cool?

00:20:30.960 --> 00:20:31.500
Yeah.

00:20:31.500 --> 00:20:33.960
Any-- yeah.

00:20:33.960 --> 00:20:37.500
Compared to the YouTube
version because this

00:20:37.500 --> 00:20:41.670
has been recorded in the past,
how different are the lectures?

00:20:41.670 --> 00:20:43.380
Oh, that's a good question.

00:20:43.380 --> 00:20:46.960
Yeah, so let me say a little bit
about what's available online.

00:20:46.960 --> 00:20:50.190
The first thing is it will
take you about four seconds

00:20:50.190 --> 00:20:54.090
to find usually weird
old versions of solutions

00:20:54.090 --> 00:20:57.700
of the homework, which
whatever, I mean,

00:20:57.700 --> 00:20:58.700
you can get them or not.

00:20:58.700 --> 00:20:59.810
That's not the point.

00:20:59.810 --> 00:21:02.260
The point of the
homework, which I would--

00:21:02.260 --> 00:21:03.890
it's actually very important.

00:21:03.890 --> 00:21:05.390
It doesn't count
for much you grade.

00:21:05.390 --> 00:21:07.120
It takes a long time.

00:21:07.120 --> 00:21:11.010
Actually, those problems all
started as final exam problems.

00:21:11.010 --> 00:21:13.780
That's where they all came from.

00:21:13.780 --> 00:21:17.140
So people at one point
said, hey, the final exam

00:21:17.140 --> 00:21:19.570
is coming up, I'm nervous,
how do I train for it.

00:21:19.570 --> 00:21:22.690
And I'm like, dude, you've
been doing final exam problems

00:21:22.690 --> 00:21:23.937
the entire quarter.

00:21:23.937 --> 00:21:25.270
What do you think the homework--

00:21:25.270 --> 00:21:28.540
I mean, we modify them a little
bit because if it's a homework

00:21:28.540 --> 00:21:31.240
problem, we can be a little
more vague and, I don't know,

00:21:31.240 --> 00:21:34.450
it could be more of a pain in
the ass, take longer to solve,

00:21:34.450 --> 00:21:36.730
that kind of thing.

00:21:36.730 --> 00:21:39.160
But the point is there's
some editing in style.

00:21:39.160 --> 00:21:44.020
But roughly speaking,
that's what they are.

00:21:44.020 --> 00:21:48.280
I mean, I kind recommend, if it
works for your learning style,

00:21:48.280 --> 00:21:51.670
working on the
homework in groups.

00:21:51.670 --> 00:21:55.280
So I mean, again, if that's
your learning style, that's

00:21:55.280 --> 00:21:56.600
a good way to do it.

00:21:56.600 --> 00:22:00.920
If you do that, collect
yourself a group of people.

00:22:00.920 --> 00:22:04.210
You do not want the people
in your group to be polite.

00:22:04.210 --> 00:22:05.300
Don't want that.

00:22:05.300 --> 00:22:07.390
So the worst thing
that could happen is--

00:22:07.390 --> 00:22:09.430
because you learn by explaining.

00:22:09.430 --> 00:22:10.610
At least that's how I learn.

00:22:10.610 --> 00:22:14.830
And so what will happen is
if you have a polite group,

00:22:14.830 --> 00:22:17.650
and you explain this, and
they go like, oh, yeah, yeah,

00:22:17.650 --> 00:22:19.570
totally, oh, yeah, sure, yeah.

00:22:19.570 --> 00:22:21.940
And what you want, you
want someone in your group

00:22:21.940 --> 00:22:26.350
that's like, sorry, I didn't
understand one thing you said.

00:22:26.350 --> 00:22:28.210
And that's a hint.

00:22:28.210 --> 00:22:30.070
I mean, if that's
all they ever say,

00:22:30.070 --> 00:22:32.590
you can kick them
out of your group.

00:22:32.590 --> 00:22:35.110
But the point is that's
actually what you want,

00:22:35.110 --> 00:22:36.760
something like that, yeah.

00:22:39.312 --> 00:22:41.770
I'll say just a little bit more
about backgrounds and stuff

00:22:41.770 --> 00:22:42.290
like that.

00:22:42.290 --> 00:22:44.590
So we have people from 20
departments in this class

00:22:44.590 --> 00:22:47.690
right now, which is really cool.

00:22:47.690 --> 00:22:49.960
So and for me, it's super
fun because basically, it

00:22:49.960 --> 00:22:52.330
is a cross-cultural experience.

00:22:52.330 --> 00:22:56.140
That's cross mathematical
cultural experience.

00:22:56.140 --> 00:22:58.750
So it's kind of fun.

00:22:58.750 --> 00:23:00.940
Maybe you do, I
don't know, genetics

00:23:00.940 --> 00:23:04.610
and the person next to you does
drones or something or somebody

00:23:04.610 --> 00:23:07.290
does machine learning, I
don't know, or economics.

00:23:07.290 --> 00:23:09.950
So it's fun.

00:23:09.950 --> 00:23:11.970
And there are few rules there.

00:23:11.970 --> 00:23:14.400
So what we do there--

00:23:14.400 --> 00:23:16.670
I mean, these are the
standard obvious rules.

00:23:16.670 --> 00:23:20.360
If you walk in a room and
basically not everyone speaks

00:23:20.360 --> 00:23:22.460
Tamil, you switch to English.

00:23:22.460 --> 00:23:25.160
I mean, hello,
that's just normal.

00:23:25.160 --> 00:23:27.530
So the same thing here.

00:23:27.530 --> 00:23:30.050
So we're going to see
a lot of different--

00:23:30.050 --> 00:23:31.460
there's a lot of
different fields

00:23:31.460 --> 00:23:33.740
that use this, a
whole lot of them.

00:23:33.740 --> 00:23:35.720
And actually, a lot
of them have dialects,

00:23:35.720 --> 00:23:37.370
including the ones I come from.

00:23:37.370 --> 00:23:39.890
I mean, strong dialects
that-- by the way,

00:23:39.890 --> 00:23:41.690
no field thinks
they have a dialect.

00:23:41.690 --> 00:23:43.250
But in fact, they all have.

00:23:43.250 --> 00:23:45.920
And some are like hick
dialects, I can tell you.

00:23:45.920 --> 00:23:48.980
Statistics has a
dialect, it does.

00:23:48.980 --> 00:23:50.450
Econ, terrible one.

00:23:50.450 --> 00:23:51.980
Oh, my God.

00:23:51.980 --> 00:23:54.780
Signal processing--
that's like physics.

00:23:54.780 --> 00:23:55.280
Oh, my God.

00:23:55.280 --> 00:23:57.260
Don't even get me
started on that.

00:23:57.260 --> 00:23:59.480
Machine learning
is just off scale.

00:23:59.480 --> 00:24:01.710
I don't even know
where that came from.

00:24:01.710 --> 00:24:03.980
I guess it's from not knowing
the basics or something.

00:24:06.680 --> 00:24:11.030
I don't know where
you would-- but I'm

00:24:11.030 --> 00:24:13.760
fluent in several of these
dialects, but not all.

00:24:13.760 --> 00:24:17.010
But I understand a lot of them,
and I would recognize them

00:24:17.010 --> 00:24:18.860
so I could intervene.

00:24:18.860 --> 00:24:21.020
But the rules apply here.

00:24:21.020 --> 00:24:23.990
If you find yourself
in a small group

00:24:23.990 --> 00:24:26.090
and everybody speaks
signal processing,

00:24:26.090 --> 00:24:28.850
feel free to switch to
signal processing dialect,

00:24:28.850 --> 00:24:31.850
by all means, or machine
learning, whatever you like.

00:24:31.850 --> 00:24:34.850
But if a normal
person shows up, you

00:24:34.850 --> 00:24:38.060
switch to what we call
hi BBC mathematics.

00:24:38.060 --> 00:24:43.130
So that means you speak at
this level using a lexicon

00:24:43.130 --> 00:24:46.460
and language that anyone
mathematically trained

00:24:46.460 --> 00:24:48.890
should understand because
that's how you go back.

00:24:48.890 --> 00:24:50.653
If I talk to someone
in finance, and they

00:24:50.653 --> 00:24:52.070
start blabbering
stuff, and I have

00:24:52.070 --> 00:24:53.528
no idea what they're
talking about,

00:24:53.528 --> 00:24:55.640
I was like, whoa,
dude, let's slow down.

00:24:55.640 --> 00:24:57.620
I'd say, what does
that even mean.

00:24:57.620 --> 00:24:59.690
And then theoretically,
we can all

00:24:59.690 --> 00:25:03.110
back up until we get to math,
and then they can say it,

00:25:03.110 --> 00:25:06.082
then they can hopefully explain
what they're trying to say.

00:25:06.082 --> 00:25:07.790
So that's kind of what
we're going to do.

00:25:07.790 --> 00:25:10.430
But every now and then, actually
just for cultural enrichment,

00:25:10.430 --> 00:25:13.610
I might mention
some dialect things.

00:25:13.610 --> 00:25:16.090
Also, that's a social
advantage for you

00:25:16.090 --> 00:25:18.590
because if you find yourself
at a party and all of a sudden,

00:25:18.590 --> 00:25:20.812
there you are with
a bunch of quants,

00:25:20.812 --> 00:25:22.520
I'll tell you a couple
of words you drop,

00:25:22.520 --> 00:25:24.720
and they'll go like,
what, like that.

00:25:24.720 --> 00:25:26.480
So that's just for fun though.

00:25:26.480 --> 00:25:30.200
That's for cultural enrichment.

00:25:30.200 --> 00:25:33.830
All right, now I think I
have covered everything.

00:25:33.830 --> 00:25:38.030
Any questions about it?

00:25:38.030 --> 00:25:42.680
So we're going to jump into
just a big overview lecture.

00:25:42.680 --> 00:25:44.150
And don't get the wrong idea.

00:25:44.150 --> 00:25:47.375
This is absolutely
not representative.

00:25:47.375 --> 00:25:48.500
First of all, I'll go fast.

00:25:48.500 --> 00:25:50.000
I won't define a
lot of terms, which

00:25:50.000 --> 00:25:51.958
is not what's going to
happen during the class.

00:25:51.958 --> 00:25:53.430
Everything I'm
about to talk about,

00:25:53.430 --> 00:25:56.510
we will talk about in
hideous detail in the next

00:25:56.510 --> 00:25:57.660
like four to five weeks.

00:25:57.660 --> 00:25:59.015
So don't worry about that.

00:26:03.020 --> 00:26:05.990
Yeah, so this is
not representative.

00:26:05.990 --> 00:26:08.330
But we'll go over it.

00:26:08.330 --> 00:26:12.410
So I'll start with just a quick
informal introduction to what

00:26:12.410 --> 00:26:14.240
is mathematical optimization.

00:26:14.240 --> 00:26:15.800
We'll talk about
actually probably

00:26:15.800 --> 00:26:19.820
the two most important
problem classes there are.

00:26:19.820 --> 00:26:22.010
Least squares is
for sure number one.

00:26:22.010 --> 00:26:25.700
And number two I think would
probably be linear programming.

00:26:25.700 --> 00:26:27.570
We'll talk about that.

00:26:27.570 --> 00:26:31.850
And then, in fact,
the parent of those

00:26:31.850 --> 00:26:33.750
is actually convex optimization.

00:26:33.750 --> 00:26:35.660
So we'll get to
that, which is more.

00:26:35.660 --> 00:26:41.060
So it's just to orient you
into what's the big picture.

00:26:41.060 --> 00:26:43.970
And I'll say a little bit,
we'll look at a little example

00:26:43.970 --> 00:26:46.430
and stuff like that, And
I'll say a little bit

00:26:46.430 --> 00:26:49.160
about a brief history of it.

00:26:49.160 --> 00:26:52.220
So here's what an
optimization problem is.

00:26:52.220 --> 00:26:57.590
And we'll go over this a lot
more in much more detail later.

00:26:57.590 --> 00:27:01.035
So now this is just a
quick fast introduction.

00:27:03.933 --> 00:27:06.100
So it's an optimization
problem, it looks like this.

00:27:06.100 --> 00:27:07.642
And there's some
critical components.

00:27:07.642 --> 00:27:09.840
Number one is you have
to have something called

00:27:09.840 --> 00:27:11.850
the optimization variable.

00:27:11.850 --> 00:27:14.580
Actually, so here's
a phrase people use

00:27:14.580 --> 00:27:17.190
in management science for
this and operations research.

00:27:17.190 --> 00:27:19.480
They call these the
decision variables,

00:27:19.480 --> 00:27:22.170
which is a fantastic name
because it tells you these are

00:27:22.170 --> 00:27:24.810
the things you need to choose.

00:27:24.810 --> 00:27:26.160
So that's decision variable.

00:27:26.160 --> 00:27:27.510
But that's dialect.

00:27:27.510 --> 00:27:33.300
So people in math would
not know what that means.

00:27:33.300 --> 00:27:37.532
So you have the variables
that you're going to choose,

00:27:37.532 --> 00:27:39.240
and then you have a
couple of components.

00:27:39.240 --> 00:27:42.660
And by the way, I actually think
of a problem in my own mind.

00:27:42.660 --> 00:27:46.470
By the way, minimize is not min.

00:27:46.470 --> 00:27:48.580
Min is a mathematical operator.

00:27:48.580 --> 00:27:50.970
It has only one definition
and has always only

00:27:50.970 --> 00:27:52.170
had one definition.

00:27:52.170 --> 00:27:55.590
It is it takes as argument
a finite set of numbers

00:27:55.590 --> 00:27:58.020
and it returns the
smallest one, period.

00:27:58.020 --> 00:27:59.520
That's what min is.

00:27:59.520 --> 00:28:01.680
Minimize is actually
a different thing.

00:28:01.680 --> 00:28:03.690
I mean, it's similar idea.

00:28:03.690 --> 00:28:06.255
I actually think of this as
a constructor for a problem.

00:28:06.255 --> 00:28:07.380
That's really what that is.

00:28:07.380 --> 00:28:09.820
So I actually think of this
in an object oriented way.

00:28:09.820 --> 00:28:12.570
And I believe that's absolutely
the correct way to do it.

00:28:12.570 --> 00:28:16.350
So an optimization
problem is actually,

00:28:16.350 --> 00:28:19.110
it's an object and an
object oriented language

00:28:19.110 --> 00:28:21.720
and it has different
attributes and fields.

00:28:21.720 --> 00:28:23.580
It's got an objective.

00:28:23.580 --> 00:28:27.000
So it's got an objective
and it's got constraints.

00:28:27.000 --> 00:28:30.460
And so let me explain a little
bit about how that works.

00:28:30.460 --> 00:28:32.070
I have a function, f0.

00:28:32.070 --> 00:28:35.040
That's going to be our
objective function here.

00:28:35.040 --> 00:28:39.467
And I mean, one
way one way people

00:28:39.467 --> 00:28:41.550
describe this I think is
a really good description

00:28:41.550 --> 00:28:44.940
actually is they call that
it's a best effort situation.

00:28:44.940 --> 00:28:49.500
Best effort means whatever value
of f0 of x you come up with,

00:28:49.500 --> 00:28:51.145
we'll accept it.

00:28:51.145 --> 00:28:53.520
By the way, the smaller it is
or the more negative it is,

00:28:53.520 --> 00:28:57.240
the happier we are,
but we'll accept it.

00:28:57.240 --> 00:29:02.940
So that's kind of just
do your best on that.

00:29:02.940 --> 00:29:06.090
Now the constraints--
these are predicates.

00:29:06.090 --> 00:29:08.400
So a predicate is a
mathematical expression

00:29:08.400 --> 00:29:10.440
that evaluates to
either true or false.

00:29:10.440 --> 00:29:12.930
So they're either
satisfied or they're not.

00:29:12.930 --> 00:29:15.330
And the predicates
describe constraints.

00:29:15.330 --> 00:29:19.350
And sometimes people call this
hard constraints or something

00:29:19.350 --> 00:29:22.920
like that because the semantics
is really, really simple.

00:29:22.920 --> 00:29:24.450
The semantics is this--

00:29:24.450 --> 00:29:28.800
if someone proposes an x and
one of these is violated,

00:29:28.800 --> 00:29:31.470
and it doesn't matter
by how much, then it

00:29:31.470 --> 00:29:34.050
is completely unacceptable.

00:29:34.050 --> 00:29:38.370
So that is the semantics
of a constraint.

00:29:38.370 --> 00:29:42.960
I mean, we'll see
where constraints

00:29:42.960 --> 00:29:45.240
come from in the wild.

00:29:45.240 --> 00:29:46.980
They can come from
different places

00:29:46.980 --> 00:29:48.390
and have different attributes.

00:29:48.390 --> 00:29:52.410
Sometimes it's a physics
thing or it's something

00:29:52.410 --> 00:29:56.280
like, actually, guess what,
covariance matrices are

00:29:56.280 --> 00:29:58.080
positive semi-definite,
so please

00:29:58.080 --> 00:29:59.970
don't give me one that's
either not symmetric

00:29:59.970 --> 00:30:03.120
or has a negative eigenvalue,
thank you, kind of thing.

00:30:03.120 --> 00:30:05.840
Or it could be just
a modest preference

00:30:05.840 --> 00:30:12.890
like please keep the leverage
on my portfolio less than 6.7.

00:30:12.890 --> 00:30:14.960
And you'd say, well,
we had a good day

00:30:14.960 --> 00:30:17.660
and some things went
up and now it's 6.8.

00:30:17.660 --> 00:30:18.770
Whatever, right?

00:30:18.770 --> 00:30:20.960
So we'll talk about those later.

00:30:20.960 --> 00:30:24.680
But that's the meaning
of a constraint.

00:30:24.680 --> 00:30:31.400
And then a solution or
optimal point x star--

00:30:31.400 --> 00:30:33.920
that's a star and not
a little asterisk.

00:30:33.920 --> 00:30:36.440
So asterisk is
going to be reserved

00:30:36.440 --> 00:30:37.970
for some other
concepts we're going

00:30:37.970 --> 00:30:41.690
to see later in the class, which
are really cool, but not now.

00:30:41.690 --> 00:30:43.970
So star sort of
means optimal here.

00:30:43.970 --> 00:30:48.500
And what it means is it has
the smallest objective value

00:30:48.500 --> 00:30:52.610
among all choices that
satisfy the constraints.

00:30:52.610 --> 00:30:54.590
So this is obvious
and you should

00:30:54.590 --> 00:30:56.000
know this anyway or something.

00:30:56.000 --> 00:30:58.000
And you've probably seen
this in lots of fields,

00:30:58.000 --> 00:30:59.420
but that's what it is.

00:30:59.420 --> 00:31:00.905
So that's an
minimization problem.

00:31:03.410 --> 00:31:04.025
There you go.

00:31:04.025 --> 00:31:05.900
Oh, and of course,
there's tons of variations

00:31:05.900 --> 00:31:06.890
which we'll look at.

00:31:06.890 --> 00:31:10.130
You could maximize something.

00:31:10.130 --> 00:31:13.520
Typically, if you maximize,
it's something like a utility

00:31:13.520 --> 00:31:18.290
or a profit or something
like that or whatever,

00:31:18.290 --> 00:31:21.950
or a score or
something like that.

00:31:21.950 --> 00:31:25.520
But we'll look at
those variations later.

00:31:25.520 --> 00:31:28.340
So let's first look at
some examples about--

00:31:28.340 --> 00:31:31.730
by the way, you'll
see tons of examples

00:31:31.730 --> 00:31:33.230
before the course is over.

00:31:35.930 --> 00:31:37.310
So I mean, here's one.

00:31:37.310 --> 00:31:39.350
Let's do portfolio optimization.

00:31:39.350 --> 00:31:43.040
So you want to construct
a portfolio of, let's say,

00:31:43.040 --> 00:31:44.900
5,000 assets.

00:31:44.900 --> 00:31:46.490
And so what that
means is you have

00:31:46.490 --> 00:31:50.330
to decide how much of
each of these do you hold.

00:31:50.330 --> 00:31:51.570
And it could be negative.

00:31:51.570 --> 00:31:52.945
That means like
a short position.

00:31:52.945 --> 00:31:55.028
And you don't have to know
what I'm talking about.

00:31:55.028 --> 00:31:55.550
It's fine.

00:31:55.550 --> 00:31:57.710
But that's what that would mean.

00:31:57.710 --> 00:31:59.900
A negative short position
means you actually

00:31:59.900 --> 00:32:02.010
borrowed it from someone,
but had the obligation

00:32:02.010 --> 00:32:03.390
to pay it back later.

00:32:03.390 --> 00:32:05.760
And that actually is
the same as owning

00:32:05.760 --> 00:32:07.890
a negative number of shares.

00:32:07.890 --> 00:32:08.932
That's a short position.

00:32:08.932 --> 00:32:10.890
Again, you don't need to
know that or whatever.

00:32:10.890 --> 00:32:11.970
You will see all this.

00:32:11.970 --> 00:32:13.680
That's just for cultural and--

00:32:13.680 --> 00:32:15.780
consider that part of
cultural enrichment

00:32:15.780 --> 00:32:18.330
if you're not in that field.

00:32:18.330 --> 00:32:22.200
So your job is to choose
how much do you put in.

00:32:22.200 --> 00:32:27.870
And presumably, there's a budget
like please invest $1 billion

00:32:27.870 --> 00:32:30.000
or something like that.

00:32:30.000 --> 00:32:33.690
There could be things like a
minimum and maximum allowed

00:32:33.690 --> 00:32:36.660
investment like please
don't put any more than 10%

00:32:36.660 --> 00:32:40.170
if your total portfolio
in any one name.

00:32:40.170 --> 00:32:46.020
I just said that in dialect, but
I think you knew what I meant.

00:32:46.020 --> 00:32:49.890
There could be a
minimum expected return.

00:32:49.890 --> 00:32:53.310
You could say, I'd like to
make at least 8% a year.

00:32:53.310 --> 00:32:57.420
At least, that's my expectation
according to my various models,

00:32:57.420 --> 00:32:59.220
something like that.

00:32:59.220 --> 00:33:01.050
The constraints
might be-- well, I

00:33:01.050 --> 00:33:05.250
mentioned one already-- a
budget, a minimum return,

00:33:05.250 --> 00:33:10.020
and the objective might be
something like the overall risk

00:33:10.020 --> 00:33:15.740
or the risk is a dialect for
the variance of the return.

00:33:15.740 --> 00:33:16.740
So that's what you'd do.

00:33:16.740 --> 00:33:19.890
So you'd say among all
portfolios with a leverage

00:33:19.890 --> 00:33:23.310
less than 3.6 with blah,
blah, blah, with this thing,

00:33:23.310 --> 00:33:28.200
with that thing, with no
positions more than 0.1 meaning

00:33:28.200 --> 00:33:31.680
whatever, you can't hold
10% in any one name,

00:33:31.680 --> 00:33:32.650
that kind of stuff.

00:33:32.650 --> 00:33:34.410
It says, among all
those, please find me

00:33:34.410 --> 00:33:36.360
the one with minimum
risk according

00:33:36.360 --> 00:33:40.020
to some risk model, which is
typically a variance model.

00:33:40.020 --> 00:33:41.050
So that's that.

00:33:47.070 --> 00:33:49.080
I am not aware of any
quantitative hedge

00:33:49.080 --> 00:33:53.140
fund that doesn't simply use
convex optimization to do this.

00:33:53.140 --> 00:33:55.290
So I'm not aware of one.

00:33:55.290 --> 00:33:57.887
They may exist, but
that's pathetic actually

00:33:57.887 --> 00:33:58.470
if you ask me.

00:33:58.470 --> 00:34:00.930
But anyway, fine.

00:34:00.930 --> 00:34:04.560
Here's another one just to
switch signs, switch areas

00:34:04.560 --> 00:34:05.260
and switch.

00:34:05.260 --> 00:34:07.770
So this device sizing
an electronic circuits.

00:34:07.770 --> 00:34:10.710
So everybody's wearing
a lot of electronics.

00:34:10.710 --> 00:34:12.000
I got a lot on me.

00:34:12.000 --> 00:34:15.870
I got a lot in front of
me and stuff like that.

00:34:15.870 --> 00:34:20.639
And when people are designing
the actual integrated circuits

00:34:20.639 --> 00:34:24.330
at the detailed inner
level, digital or analog,

00:34:24.330 --> 00:34:27.389
there's a point where people
do analog size-- actually,

00:34:27.389 --> 00:34:30.150
this optimization is used
throughout circuit design,

00:34:30.150 --> 00:34:31.409
throughout.

00:34:31.409 --> 00:34:36.570
So maybe not for super
hardcore small analog stuff

00:34:36.570 --> 00:34:39.659
where it's still kind
of old Cowboys coming up

00:34:39.659 --> 00:34:43.350
with a weird circuit
from 1927 or something

00:34:43.350 --> 00:34:47.460
that was vacuum tubes and
now it's in whatever CMOS.

00:34:47.460 --> 00:34:51.730
But for the stuff that
you have around you,

00:34:51.730 --> 00:34:53.409
sizing is a big deal.

00:34:53.409 --> 00:34:57.190
And so the idea there is, I
might have a logic block inside

00:34:57.190 --> 00:34:59.380
a chip where-- and again,
you don't have to--

00:34:59.380 --> 00:35:02.410
when we talk about these
things for real, I'll say more.

00:35:02.410 --> 00:35:05.050
But now, it's just should
just get the flavor of it.

00:35:05.050 --> 00:35:07.120
So I have a logic
block inside a chip,

00:35:07.120 --> 00:35:11.740
I have, let's say, I don't
know, a million gates inside.

00:35:11.740 --> 00:35:14.782
And its job is really simple--

00:35:14.782 --> 00:35:16.240
a bunch of ones
and zeros are going

00:35:16.240 --> 00:35:19.360
to come active at the
input to this block

00:35:19.360 --> 00:35:23.860
and some number of
picoseconds later,

00:35:23.860 --> 00:35:26.770
we hope that whatever
that block computes

00:35:26.770 --> 00:35:28.810
is a bunch of ones
and zeros that it's

00:35:28.810 --> 00:35:32.092
going to come out by the
deadline on the right hand side

00:35:32.092 --> 00:35:34.300
where it's going to be
latched into some new latches.

00:35:34.300 --> 00:35:36.508
And again, if you know what
I'm talking about, great.

00:35:36.508 --> 00:35:39.040
If you don't, that's fine, too.

00:35:39.040 --> 00:35:41.350
So that's a timing
requirement and it's

00:35:41.350 --> 00:35:42.760
unbelievably important.

00:35:42.760 --> 00:35:45.070
Or here would be the slang--

00:35:45.070 --> 00:35:50.350
I want to make sure this can
clock at 2.8ghz, this core.

00:35:50.350 --> 00:35:52.070
So that would be the slang.

00:35:52.070 --> 00:35:54.980
But that translates into
a timing requirement.

00:35:54.980 --> 00:35:57.500
That's what it means.

00:35:57.500 --> 00:36:01.700
Now the basic idea is that for
each of your million gates,

00:36:01.700 --> 00:36:05.360
let's say, you can
actually change its size.

00:36:05.360 --> 00:36:07.550
If you make it big,
here's the good news--

00:36:07.550 --> 00:36:08.990
it's fast.

00:36:08.990 --> 00:36:10.860
You make them big, they're fast.

00:36:10.860 --> 00:36:11.360
Why?

00:36:11.360 --> 00:36:13.730
Because a lot of current
can come in and charge up

00:36:13.730 --> 00:36:16.190
the capacitance fast and
it's going to be fast.

00:36:16.190 --> 00:36:17.420
That's the good news.

00:36:17.420 --> 00:36:22.340
Bad news-- it's big physically
and you've got a limit on area.

00:36:22.340 --> 00:36:26.960
Other bad news is
it's power hungry.

00:36:26.960 --> 00:36:31.220
And actually, power is kind of
the limiting thing right now

00:36:31.220 --> 00:36:33.980
in a lot of circuit design.

00:36:33.980 --> 00:36:35.660
So now the idea is--

00:36:35.660 --> 00:36:39.380
I mean, it's obvious
what to do intuitively--

00:36:42.320 --> 00:36:43.310
here are some slang--

00:36:43.310 --> 00:36:46.580
for the critical paths,
those are the ones that says,

00:36:46.580 --> 00:36:50.210
I'll tell you what,
to clock at 2.8ghz,

00:36:50.210 --> 00:36:53.210
your hardest thing is going to
be the signal that flows from

00:36:53.210 --> 00:36:55.040
these things over
to those over there.

00:36:55.040 --> 00:36:56.510
Those are the critical paths.

00:36:56.510 --> 00:36:59.240
You're going to want to
size those big because you

00:36:59.240 --> 00:37:02.000
want to make sure that
with high probability,

00:37:02.000 --> 00:37:05.775
the signal is valid when the
next clock cycle comes around.

00:37:05.775 --> 00:37:07.400
And for things that
are less so, you're

00:37:07.400 --> 00:37:09.740
going to want to set
them to minimum size.

00:37:09.740 --> 00:37:13.280
So I mean, this is
kind of obvious,

00:37:13.280 --> 00:37:17.990
but this is actually all
done by optimization.

00:37:17.990 --> 00:37:19.960
So all that kind of makes sense?

00:37:23.200 --> 00:37:24.250
So that's one.

00:37:24.250 --> 00:37:26.920
And then the last example
is kind of hilarious

00:37:26.920 --> 00:37:30.070
because it subsumes pretty much
most of statistics and machine

00:37:30.070 --> 00:37:31.150
learning.

00:37:31.150 --> 00:37:34.120
It's just called data fitting.

00:37:34.120 --> 00:37:37.690
And it's this--
here, the variables

00:37:37.690 --> 00:37:40.090
are parameters in a model.

00:37:40.090 --> 00:37:43.090
Could be a statistical model,
a prediction model, whatever.

00:37:43.090 --> 00:37:45.820
It's a model of something
and you got parameters.

00:37:45.820 --> 00:37:47.800
If you're doing line of
old school statistics,

00:37:47.800 --> 00:37:48.910
you might have 10.

00:37:48.910 --> 00:37:51.490
And if you're training
a neural network,

00:37:51.490 --> 00:37:54.910
you might have 10 billion.

00:37:54.910 --> 00:37:57.910
But those are the variables
in an optimization problem.

00:37:57.910 --> 00:38:02.980
And then the constraints might
be based on prior information

00:38:02.980 --> 00:38:06.760
like if you're estimating,
for example, an intensity,

00:38:06.760 --> 00:38:07.780
it's got to be positive.

00:38:07.780 --> 00:38:08.980
It can't be negative.

00:38:08.980 --> 00:38:10.840
If you're estimating
a covariance matrix,

00:38:10.840 --> 00:38:12.550
it's got to be
positive semidefinite.

00:38:12.550 --> 00:38:16.400
So these are like constraints.

00:38:16.400 --> 00:38:17.060
Let's see.

00:38:17.060 --> 00:38:21.090
And your objective typically
involves two things.

00:38:21.090 --> 00:38:26.180
The first is a measure of
misfit with the data you have,

00:38:26.180 --> 00:38:28.250
the training data.

00:38:28.250 --> 00:38:29.360
So that's the first thing.

00:38:29.360 --> 00:38:30.860
I someone says, what are you
doing, you say, I'm fitting

00:38:30.860 --> 00:38:31.460
a model.

00:38:31.460 --> 00:38:33.810
They go, how are you doing
it, and you go, well, I mean,

00:38:33.810 --> 00:38:37.270
the first thing is I want it
to reproduce the data I have.

00:38:37.270 --> 00:38:40.900
Then the critical part because
if you're not doing well

00:38:40.900 --> 00:38:43.570
on the historical data,
you should just give up

00:38:43.570 --> 00:38:44.800
or something like that.

00:38:44.800 --> 00:38:46.960
Time to go home.

00:38:46.960 --> 00:38:48.880
But then there's another term.

00:38:48.880 --> 00:38:51.460
And someone says, well,
what's that other term.

00:38:51.460 --> 00:38:55.420
And you go, ah, that's a
regularization or a detuning

00:38:55.420 --> 00:38:57.850
term or in
optimization, you call

00:38:57.850 --> 00:39:00.423
it a term that
promotes robustness.

00:39:00.423 --> 00:39:01.840
And someone says,
oh, what's that.

00:39:01.840 --> 00:39:04.060
And you say, wait, do you
mean you're not actually

00:39:04.060 --> 00:39:06.250
choosing the parameters
to do the best

00:39:06.250 --> 00:39:07.360
on the historical data.

00:39:07.360 --> 00:39:09.638
And you say, right,
exactly, right,

00:39:09.638 --> 00:39:11.930
because I don't want to
overfit or something like that.

00:39:11.930 --> 00:39:14.110
And I'm sure you've
seen all these ideas.

00:39:14.110 --> 00:39:17.350
But that's another example where
people would use optimization.

00:39:17.350 --> 00:39:20.980
What's super interesting is
just to think a little bit

00:39:20.980 --> 00:39:26.740
about how the variables enter
in these three broad categories.

00:39:26.740 --> 00:39:30.010
Here, it's actually
prescriptive.

00:39:30.010 --> 00:39:32.710
When you calculate this, you
get a trade list and it says,

00:39:32.710 --> 00:39:37.190
go buy so many shares of
this, dump that, buy this, buy

00:39:37.190 --> 00:39:38.767
that, that kind of thing.

00:39:38.767 --> 00:39:39.600
That's prescription.

00:39:39.600 --> 00:39:41.150
So things actually happen.

00:39:41.150 --> 00:39:43.820
I mean, another good example
of that would be control.

00:39:43.820 --> 00:39:46.250
So that's another
big huge example here

00:39:46.250 --> 00:39:50.180
that would be like landing
a rocket, flying a rocket,

00:39:50.180 --> 00:39:53.690
flying an airplane,
autonomous driving.

00:39:53.690 --> 00:39:56.900
There, it's prescriptive.

00:39:56.900 --> 00:40:00.620
Here are the
optimization variables.

00:40:00.620 --> 00:40:02.990
It's actually literally
the four thrusts

00:40:02.990 --> 00:40:07.260
on your drone in 20 millisecond
increments for the next 10

00:40:07.260 --> 00:40:07.760
seconds.

00:40:07.760 --> 00:40:09.650
And things happen.

00:40:09.650 --> 00:40:12.560
When you come up with
an x or whatever it is,

00:40:12.560 --> 00:40:15.890
you download it in your
drone and it flies.

00:40:15.890 --> 00:40:17.570
So same here.

00:40:17.570 --> 00:40:19.670
Here, I mean, things happen.

00:40:19.670 --> 00:40:22.370
It's just on a much
smaller time scale--

00:40:22.370 --> 00:40:25.700
sorry-- longer time scale,
much longer time scale.

00:40:25.700 --> 00:40:28.135
So electronic design--
people design bridges.

00:40:28.135 --> 00:40:30.260
This is how people design
bridges and big buildings

00:40:30.260 --> 00:40:32.840
and steel structures
and things like that.

00:40:32.840 --> 00:40:36.500
You spend a lot of time working
it out and simulating it

00:40:36.500 --> 00:40:39.100
like crazy, and then
you build the thing,

00:40:39.100 --> 00:40:40.850
and you're hoping it's
going to stay there

00:40:40.850 --> 00:40:42.800
for hundreds years or 200
years, that kind of thing.

00:40:42.800 --> 00:40:43.790
I mean, not for a circuit.

00:40:43.790 --> 00:40:44.707
That's for a building.

00:40:44.707 --> 00:40:46.280
For a circuit,
you're hoping it's

00:40:46.280 --> 00:40:50.970
going to last four or five
years, something like that.

00:40:50.970 --> 00:40:52.940
And then in data
fitting, the variables

00:40:52.940 --> 00:40:55.910
are not actions at all.

00:40:55.910 --> 00:40:58.790
Nothing happens if I
show you a coefficient

00:40:58.790 --> 00:41:00.650
in a regression
model or something.

00:41:00.650 --> 00:41:02.030
You don't do something.

00:41:02.030 --> 00:41:03.840
It's just a parameter.

00:41:03.840 --> 00:41:05.900
So it's interesting
to think about this.

00:41:05.900 --> 00:41:08.600
But there's a lot of other
applications of optimization

00:41:08.600 --> 00:41:12.020
which we will see that aren't--
but these are just kind

00:41:12.020 --> 00:41:15.095
of good, basic, simple examples.

00:41:21.130 --> 00:41:24.100
So you might ask--
yeah, by the way,

00:41:24.100 --> 00:41:26.680
it's very normal when people
learn about this first

00:41:26.680 --> 00:41:30.340
is to wake up sweating a little
bit in the middle of the night

00:41:30.340 --> 00:41:33.700
and you're like, oh, my God,
everything is an optimization

00:41:33.700 --> 00:41:36.640
problem, oh, my God.

00:41:36.640 --> 00:41:38.350
So should this
happen to you-- it's

00:41:38.350 --> 00:41:40.725
going to happen in one or two
of you, I can guarantee it,

00:41:40.725 --> 00:41:41.560
just statistically.

00:41:41.560 --> 00:41:45.280
So just calm down.

00:41:45.280 --> 00:41:47.710
Yes, duh, everything is
an optimization problem.

00:41:47.710 --> 00:41:48.550
Of course, it is.

00:41:48.550 --> 00:41:52.000
Then not only it's both
true and says nothing.

00:41:52.000 --> 00:41:54.100
It's like a tautology.

00:41:54.100 --> 00:41:54.700
Of course.

00:41:59.290 --> 00:42:03.400
So then the question is, once
you realize what's everything,

00:42:03.400 --> 00:42:06.020
it's absolutely everything,
it's all of design,

00:42:06.020 --> 00:42:09.220
it's all of statistics, it's
all of blah, blah, blah.

00:42:09.220 --> 00:42:11.320
OK, fine.

00:42:11.320 --> 00:42:14.320
Then you ask yourself, OK, cool,
now that's really interesting

00:42:14.320 --> 00:42:15.310
now, can you solve it.

00:42:15.310 --> 00:42:18.700
And now we get to the
bad news, which is no,

00:42:18.700 --> 00:42:19.930
generally you can't.

00:42:19.930 --> 00:42:22.060
So sorry, yeah.

00:42:22.060 --> 00:42:24.520
Yeah, so that's OK.

00:42:24.520 --> 00:42:28.780
And there's different types
of ways to get around that.

00:42:28.780 --> 00:42:31.900
There's two traditional
compromises,

00:42:31.900 --> 00:42:36.880
one taken 99.9% of the
time and 1.1% of the time.

00:42:36.880 --> 00:42:44.290
The first compromise
is to actually solve,

00:42:44.290 --> 00:42:45.790
and then you have
a little asterisk.

00:42:45.790 --> 00:42:49.150
And then down at the footnote
in extremely small font,

00:42:49.150 --> 00:42:54.430
it says, basically not really
or something like that.

00:42:54.430 --> 00:42:56.740
And I'm not making fun
of this, but I'm just

00:42:56.740 --> 00:43:00.250
saying that a whole
lot of people do this.

00:43:00.250 --> 00:43:01.940
And it's fine.

00:43:01.940 --> 00:43:07.252
It just means that you found
a point that was feasible,

00:43:07.252 --> 00:43:09.460
it has a better-- let's say
it has a better objective

00:43:09.460 --> 00:43:11.780
value than what you started
with, and you're happy.

00:43:11.780 --> 00:43:14.300
But is it the absolute
best anyone can do?

00:43:14.300 --> 00:43:15.860
You don't know.

00:43:15.860 --> 00:43:18.290
I mean, if you're honest.

00:43:18.290 --> 00:43:19.790
That's called
local optimization.

00:43:19.790 --> 00:43:23.900
It's very widely used,
there's nothing wrong with it,

00:43:23.900 --> 00:43:28.280
it's fine as long as you don't
pretend that you've actually

00:43:28.280 --> 00:43:29.390
solved a problem.

00:43:29.390 --> 00:43:31.527
Then we get into trouble.

00:43:31.527 --> 00:43:33.860
And there's lots of cases
where that doesn't even really

00:43:33.860 --> 00:43:34.230
matter.

00:43:34.230 --> 00:43:34.980
I mean, it's fine.

00:43:34.980 --> 00:43:37.040
So actually, right
now, as we speak,

00:43:37.040 --> 00:43:41.060
there's 10 million local
optimization, maybe more than

00:43:41.060 --> 00:43:43.650
that, local optimization
problems are running right now.

00:43:43.650 --> 00:43:46.280
So someone want to
tell me what they are?

00:43:48.863 --> 00:43:51.280
What are local optimizations
running right now, 10 million

00:43:51.280 --> 00:43:55.320
of them, minimum 10 million?

00:43:55.320 --> 00:43:59.550
On GPUs, all over
the world, in other--

00:43:59.550 --> 00:44:02.080
people are fitting-- go ahead.

00:44:02.080 --> 00:44:02.880
Neural network.

00:44:02.880 --> 00:44:03.512
Oh, yeah.

00:44:03.512 --> 00:44:05.220
People are fitting
neural networks, yeah.

00:44:05.220 --> 00:44:06.810
So there you go.

00:44:06.810 --> 00:44:10.650
And they don't care that--

00:44:10.650 --> 00:44:13.590
no one comes up to you and
says, wow, that's awesome,

00:44:13.590 --> 00:44:15.330
is that the absolute
best you could do.

00:44:15.330 --> 00:44:16.680
And by the way, the
reason you might not

00:44:16.680 --> 00:44:18.805
care-- there's a lot of
reasons you might not care.

00:44:18.805 --> 00:44:20.730
You say, number one, I
made up the objective.

00:44:20.730 --> 00:44:21.780
That's the first thing.

00:44:21.780 --> 00:44:24.240
So we shouldn't get
too crazy about that.

00:44:24.240 --> 00:44:27.000
Number two, the objective
is merely a surrogate

00:44:27.000 --> 00:44:28.680
for what I really want to do.

00:44:28.680 --> 00:44:29.610
And you go, what do
you really want to do.

00:44:29.610 --> 00:44:31.650
What I really want to
do is make excellent day

00:44:31.650 --> 00:44:32.610
ahead predictions.

00:44:32.610 --> 00:44:34.140
That's what I really want to do.

00:44:34.140 --> 00:44:36.570
All this optimization
stuff, all this machine

00:44:36.570 --> 00:44:39.700
learning optimization crap--
that's just a way to get there.

00:44:39.700 --> 00:44:43.415
So you're not paid to
minimize loss functions.

00:44:43.415 --> 00:44:44.790
If you're doing
machine learning,

00:44:44.790 --> 00:44:48.090
you're paid to do a good
job on tomorrow's data,

00:44:48.090 --> 00:44:49.980
on unseen data.

00:44:49.980 --> 00:44:53.280
So there's lots of reasons
why you could say you couldn't

00:44:53.280 --> 00:44:55.670
care less in many, many cases.

00:44:55.670 --> 00:44:58.220
And that's all perfectly valid.

00:44:58.220 --> 00:44:59.975
Now there are some exceptions.

00:44:59.975 --> 00:45:01.350
And I'm going to
talk about them.

00:45:01.350 --> 00:45:03.770
And that's actually kind of
what this course is about.

00:45:03.770 --> 00:45:07.040
So the exceptions are there
are certain kind of problems

00:45:07.040 --> 00:45:08.510
that you can just solve.

00:45:08.510 --> 00:45:10.940
There's no art of
any kind whatsoever.

00:45:10.940 --> 00:45:11.690
It's a technology.

00:45:11.690 --> 00:45:12.800
You just solve it.

00:45:12.800 --> 00:45:14.990
I mean, if you push these
things to the extremes

00:45:14.990 --> 00:45:17.540
and blah, blah, blah, there
might be some minimal art.

00:45:17.540 --> 00:45:19.940
But fine.

00:45:19.940 --> 00:45:22.370
And the most famous one
is least squares problem.

00:45:22.370 --> 00:45:23.270
I'll talk about that.

00:45:23.270 --> 00:45:26.870
That goes back to officially
to about year 1,800,

00:45:26.870 --> 00:45:28.460
but it's almost
certain that people

00:45:28.460 --> 00:45:30.560
knew about it way before that.

00:45:30.560 --> 00:45:33.958
And I'll say a lot about it.

00:45:33.958 --> 00:45:35.750
Another one is linear
programming problems.

00:45:35.750 --> 00:45:37.542
And of course, convex
optimization problems

00:45:37.542 --> 00:45:38.790
is another example.

00:45:38.790 --> 00:45:43.220
Now for these things,
for these problems,

00:45:43.220 --> 00:45:47.390
when you say you've solved
it, there's no asterisk.

00:45:47.390 --> 00:45:53.420
You don't pull in the legal
boilerplate, the release

00:45:53.420 --> 00:45:55.790
that when you read in
small writing or the thing

00:45:55.790 --> 00:45:57.110
you said when you said agree.

00:45:57.110 --> 00:45:59.360
It was like way, way down
where you didn't read it.

00:45:59.360 --> 00:46:01.100
It said things we
understand that this may not

00:46:01.100 --> 00:46:03.767
be the best x, blah, blah, blah,
blah, blah, that kind of thing.

00:46:03.767 --> 00:46:06.690
But that's what this is.

00:46:06.690 --> 00:46:07.820
They just work.

00:46:07.820 --> 00:46:09.800
By the way, what's super
interesting about that

00:46:09.800 --> 00:46:13.280
is that makes them
really suitable

00:46:13.280 --> 00:46:17.050
for embedded applications.

00:46:17.050 --> 00:46:23.860
So I was joking with a friend,
if you got on a flight,

00:46:23.860 --> 00:46:28.037
and the captain said,
great news, today, we're

00:46:28.037 --> 00:46:29.620
going to see some
interesting weather,

00:46:29.620 --> 00:46:33.310
and we're going to be
training our RL controller,

00:46:33.310 --> 00:46:35.560
it's going to be real fun.

00:46:35.560 --> 00:46:37.180
I mean, this is ridiculous.

00:46:40.180 --> 00:46:43.300
So actually, a lot of jet
engines work this way.

00:46:43.300 --> 00:46:45.310
You actually solve an
optimization problem

00:46:45.310 --> 00:46:46.840
50 times a second.

00:46:46.840 --> 00:46:48.730
They're really
complicated, a jet engine.

00:46:48.730 --> 00:46:50.830
There's like 50
different things.

00:46:50.830 --> 00:46:54.940
The x that you're choosing is
like at least dimension 20.

00:46:54.940 --> 00:46:58.090
It's like all sorts of crazy
little bleed valves and things

00:46:58.090 --> 00:47:01.130
and you can change the
angle of attack on wrote.

00:47:01.130 --> 00:47:03.010
It's just crazy stuff.

00:47:03.010 --> 00:47:05.150
Fuel mix, all sorts
of crazy stuff

00:47:05.150 --> 00:47:06.760
you can do to run a jet engine.

00:47:06.760 --> 00:47:08.500
They're very serious.

00:47:08.500 --> 00:47:11.890
Here's what happens-- actually
solves a convex problem

00:47:11.890 --> 00:47:14.530
50 times a second.

00:47:14.530 --> 00:47:17.730
Notice I said, not
a neural network.

00:47:17.730 --> 00:47:22.160
So nevertheless, and that's
because these things,

00:47:22.160 --> 00:47:23.537
they just work.

00:47:23.537 --> 00:47:25.370
So they're very good
for that kind of stuff.

00:47:27.950 --> 00:47:31.370
But it's probably better not
to get all weird about it

00:47:31.370 --> 00:47:35.300
and say things like,
oh, what are you doing,

00:47:35.300 --> 00:47:38.150
I'm solving a convex problem, I
get the actual global solution,

00:47:38.150 --> 00:47:43.010
blah, blah, blah, blah, blah,
and, oh, wow, look at you, oh,

00:47:43.010 --> 00:47:45.590
is that stochastic
gradient descent, oh, OK.

00:47:45.590 --> 00:47:49.070
It's better not to get
too bent out of shape

00:47:49.070 --> 00:47:51.440
about all these things.

00:47:51.440 --> 00:47:53.370
So let's start
with least squares.

00:47:53.370 --> 00:47:57.590
So like I said, least squares
goes back to around 1,800

00:47:57.590 --> 00:48:00.603
with Gauss and Legendre.

00:48:00.603 --> 00:48:01.520
Maybe it was Lagrange.

00:48:01.520 --> 00:48:03.950
I can't remember who
was involved in this.

00:48:03.950 --> 00:48:05.990
And that's, of course,
the Western mythology.

00:48:05.990 --> 00:48:08.690
So almost certainly, it
was known in India or China

00:48:08.690 --> 00:48:12.110
for sure and in various
other ancient places.

00:48:12.110 --> 00:48:13.100
Persia for sure.

00:48:15.860 --> 00:48:19.603
Anyway, so it has a
least squares solution.

00:48:19.603 --> 00:48:21.770
By the way, because it has
a least squares solution,

00:48:21.770 --> 00:48:24.560
that means it's
taught by people who

00:48:24.560 --> 00:48:27.110
have this kind of 19th
century math training, which

00:48:27.110 --> 00:48:29.810
is most people, who teach.

00:48:29.810 --> 00:48:32.690
You write down the formula
and a lot of paper and pencil

00:48:32.690 --> 00:48:34.483
kind of stuff just
like their parents did

00:48:34.483 --> 00:48:36.650
and their grandparents and
their great grandparents,

00:48:36.650 --> 00:48:37.190
that kind of stuff.

00:48:37.190 --> 00:48:38.960
So it's that kind of--
it's fine and we shouldn't

00:48:38.960 --> 00:48:40.877
make too much fun of it
because a lot of stuff

00:48:40.877 --> 00:48:43.580
was built that way.

00:48:43.580 --> 00:48:47.600
So it's just an analytical
solution if A has full rank

00:48:47.600 --> 00:48:49.172
and is tall.

00:48:49.172 --> 00:48:51.380
And there's lots of reliable
and efficient algorithms

00:48:51.380 --> 00:48:54.230
and software to do this.

00:48:54.230 --> 00:48:56.540
And basically, you can
solve this in a time which

00:48:56.540 --> 00:48:57.890
is proportional to--

00:48:57.890 --> 00:49:00.260
actually, this is a
theme that you will

00:49:00.260 --> 00:49:02.150
later in the class very well.

00:49:02.150 --> 00:49:05.120
It's the big time
small squared theme.

00:49:05.120 --> 00:49:06.770
There's two dimensions here.

00:49:06.770 --> 00:49:08.930
There's the number of rows.

00:49:08.930 --> 00:49:11.370
That's the number of terms in
your least squares problem.

00:49:11.370 --> 00:49:13.280
And is the dimension
of x, that's

00:49:13.280 --> 00:49:18.530
the number of variables
you have, which is n here.

00:49:18.530 --> 00:49:24.440
And the computation time
for this is nothing.

00:49:24.440 --> 00:49:27.050
And if A is structured
like it's sparse,

00:49:27.050 --> 00:49:28.970
you can do this even faster.

00:49:28.970 --> 00:49:31.040
But you can do these at
just stunning speeds.

00:49:31.040 --> 00:49:36.050
I'm sure people here know
it's absolutely not--

00:49:36.050 --> 00:49:45.500
it is nothing to solve a
problem with 10 million entries

00:49:45.500 --> 00:49:47.720
and a couple of hundred
parameters or, by the way,

00:49:47.720 --> 00:49:49.550
the other way around
with regularization.

00:49:49.550 --> 00:49:52.100
It's just absolutely nothing.

00:49:52.100 --> 00:49:54.110
I can do that on a laptop.

00:49:54.110 --> 00:49:56.642
And then what can
we do on a GPU?

00:49:56.642 --> 00:49:57.725
Don't even get me started.

00:50:00.320 --> 00:50:04.610
So these things are
really pretty crazy.

00:50:04.610 --> 00:50:06.500
So that's a mature technology.

00:50:08.838 --> 00:50:10.880
So the question is, how
do you use least squares.

00:50:10.880 --> 00:50:12.422
It's actually a
really good question.

00:50:12.422 --> 00:50:15.140
You could teach a super
awesome two week short course

00:50:15.140 --> 00:50:18.410
on it, which is basically
like that's least squares,

00:50:18.410 --> 00:50:19.758
we can solve them, end of story.

00:50:19.758 --> 00:50:21.800
You don't do the silly
theory and stuff like that

00:50:21.800 --> 00:50:23.630
because it's totally irrelevant.

00:50:23.630 --> 00:50:25.860
So you just say that's
it, it's a fact,

00:50:25.860 --> 00:50:28.220
we can solve ginormous
least squares problems.

00:50:28.220 --> 00:50:30.140
You can right now.

00:50:30.140 --> 00:50:32.240
You don't even need torch to do.

00:50:32.240 --> 00:50:33.990
You can just solve
just gigantic problems.

00:50:33.990 --> 00:50:35.698
And then you'd say,
I'm going to show you

00:50:35.698 --> 00:50:37.790
some street fighting
tricks for least squares,

00:50:37.790 --> 00:50:39.530
and you will be empowered.

00:50:39.530 --> 00:50:42.980
So this course doesn't exist,
but it'd be awesome if it did.

00:50:42.980 --> 00:50:46.040
And there's just like you learn
about three or four tricks,

00:50:46.040 --> 00:50:48.530
and you are good to go.

00:50:48.530 --> 00:50:50.330
The tricks are
things like throwing

00:50:50.330 --> 00:50:54.530
in weights on different
data entries, which

00:50:54.530 --> 00:50:58.490
is silly, regularization
and adding terms.

00:50:58.490 --> 00:51:00.062
You'd say, oh, I
like the trajectory

00:51:00.062 --> 00:51:01.520
I got from my
airplane, but there's

00:51:01.520 --> 00:51:02.562
too much rudder movement.

00:51:02.562 --> 00:51:05.810
And you're like, fine, throw
in an additional least squares

00:51:05.810 --> 00:51:07.140
term on rudder movement.

00:51:07.140 --> 00:51:09.140
So these are the kinds
of street fighting things

00:51:09.140 --> 00:51:11.480
people do and it's
really pretty cool.

00:51:11.480 --> 00:51:13.790
So everybody got this?

00:51:13.790 --> 00:51:17.230
So you learn a handful of
tricks in least squares

00:51:17.230 --> 00:51:21.910
and you are you are good to go
in a lot of practical problems.

00:51:21.910 --> 00:51:23.800
We should also
not make fun of it

00:51:23.800 --> 00:51:27.370
