00:53:20.040 --> 00:53:22.770
All right, so linear
programming-- so I'm

00:53:22.770 --> 00:53:24.570
from a more
mathematical background,

00:53:24.570 --> 00:53:27.840
and you didn't hear
about it because there's

00:53:27.840 --> 00:53:30.270
no analytical formula for it.

00:53:30.270 --> 00:53:32.280
Even worse, most
of the algorithms

00:53:32.280 --> 00:53:34.770
that work for it, no
one can explain why.

00:53:34.770 --> 00:53:37.242
I mean, they can, but it's
like 100 pages of dense math.

00:53:37.242 --> 00:53:39.450
And I don't even know if I
believe that that actually

00:53:39.450 --> 00:53:40.860
explains why it works.

00:53:40.860 --> 00:53:44.520
So there's some
weird snobbery there.

00:53:44.520 --> 00:53:46.980
But linear programming is
just the dumbest thing ever.

00:53:46.980 --> 00:53:48.840
It says, minimize
a linear function

00:53:48.840 --> 00:53:51.150
subject to a bunch
of constraints

00:53:51.150 --> 00:53:53.250
that are linear inequalities.

00:53:53.250 --> 00:53:54.750
And so the classic.

00:53:54.750 --> 00:53:57.690
We're going to go over
this in hideous detail.

00:53:57.690 --> 00:54:00.270
But it would look
something like this--

00:54:00.270 --> 00:54:03.960
if these are the
constraint sets,

00:54:03.960 --> 00:54:06.840
it basically it says you
have to stay in here.

00:54:06.840 --> 00:54:08.880
That's the feasible set.

00:54:08.880 --> 00:54:10.650
And then there'll
be an objective.

00:54:10.650 --> 00:54:14.700
And the objective will
be this is C, let's say.

00:54:14.700 --> 00:54:18.160
And so it says, please go as far
as possible in this direction.

00:54:18.160 --> 00:54:20.430
And so the solution is here.

00:54:20.430 --> 00:54:24.670
And we're going to go
over this in great detail.

00:54:24.670 --> 00:54:25.890
This is fine.

00:54:25.890 --> 00:54:29.790
But the main point
is I can do this

00:54:29.790 --> 00:54:31.530
when the dimension
of x is a million

00:54:31.530 --> 00:54:34.350
and the number of
constraints is 10 million.

00:54:34.350 --> 00:54:35.710
And we can just do it.

00:54:35.710 --> 00:54:38.040
It's just a non problem.

00:54:38.040 --> 00:54:41.640
By the way, if I have a
small linear program with,

00:54:41.640 --> 00:54:45.240
let's say, I don't know,
let's say 1,000 variables

00:54:45.240 --> 00:54:48.120
and 5,000 linear inequality
constraints-- everybody

00:54:48.120 --> 00:54:49.260
picturing this?

00:54:49.260 --> 00:54:51.390
So by the way, you
can't picture it.

00:54:51.390 --> 00:54:52.710
Duh, hello, you can't.

00:54:52.710 --> 00:54:54.150
But you can pretend to.

00:54:54.150 --> 00:54:55.600
And that's what we do.

00:54:55.600 --> 00:54:57.420
So we're going to
pretend to visualize

00:54:57.420 --> 00:55:01.800
an R 1,000, 5,000 half spaces.

00:55:01.800 --> 00:55:05.280
So now what you're seeing
is this big old polyhedron

00:55:05.280 --> 00:55:08.700
in R 1,000.

00:55:08.700 --> 00:55:10.530
Now it's kind of
obvious from this

00:55:10.530 --> 00:55:14.130
that you can't find a
solution on a vertex, which

00:55:14.130 --> 00:55:16.290
is one of these corner points.

00:55:16.290 --> 00:55:18.330
So then the question
is, well, I just

00:55:18.330 --> 00:55:20.160
have to look at the vertices.

00:55:20.160 --> 00:55:22.020
This thing has whatever--

00:55:22.020 --> 00:55:23.610
1, 2, 3-- 4 vertices.

00:55:23.610 --> 00:55:26.550
We just check each one and find
out which has the best value.

00:55:26.550 --> 00:55:30.780
So how many vertices
does a polyhedron

00:55:30.780 --> 00:55:37.410
with 1,000 variables defined
by 5,000 inequalities have?

00:55:37.410 --> 00:55:45.290
I mean just roughly
like 1,000, 5,000.

00:55:45.290 --> 00:55:47.940
Roughly.

00:55:47.940 --> 00:55:50.130
Just guessing, just guessing.

00:55:50.130 --> 00:55:54.490
So the answer is it's
about 5,000 choose 1,000.

00:55:54.490 --> 00:56:00.370
That's a really, really big
number just for the record.

00:56:00.370 --> 00:56:02.560
I can solve that problem
in about 50 milliseconds

00:56:02.560 --> 00:56:05.260
on my laptop.

00:56:05.260 --> 00:56:08.860
And you're like,
whoa, you actually--

00:56:08.860 --> 00:56:11.980
and you can make it sound
all fancy and stuff when

00:56:11.980 --> 00:56:14.590
you explain what you've
actually done, but that's it.

00:56:14.590 --> 00:56:16.060
So anyway, it's pretty cool.

00:56:19.660 --> 00:56:24.935
So there's really good
different methods to do this.

00:56:24.935 --> 00:56:26.810
As a matter of fact, by
the end of the class,

00:56:26.810 --> 00:56:30.820
you'll probably implement
your own only to demystify it.

00:56:30.820 --> 00:56:32.390
That's the only reason.

00:56:32.390 --> 00:56:34.540
Otherwise, you go
through your entire life

00:56:34.540 --> 00:56:36.880
with people telling you
like, Oh, my God, it's

00:56:36.880 --> 00:56:39.550
extremely hard to solve a
linear program, oh, my God,

00:56:39.550 --> 00:56:42.220
you have to use this
commercial software,

00:56:42.220 --> 00:56:44.950
get out your Visa card and pay.

00:56:44.950 --> 00:56:48.400
Then you'll be able to say,
dude, I implemented one

00:56:48.400 --> 00:56:49.990
on a homework.

00:56:49.990 --> 00:56:51.220
So anyway, fine.

00:56:51.220 --> 00:56:52.600
And it was fine, thank you.

00:56:56.530 --> 00:56:59.140
By the way, once again,
you get the weird big time

00:56:59.140 --> 00:57:01.600
small squared thing.

00:57:01.600 --> 00:57:04.630
If there's 100 variables
and 10,000 inequalities,

00:57:04.630 --> 00:57:08.170
the complexity is that.

00:57:08.170 --> 00:57:10.360
So it's a mature technology.

00:57:10.360 --> 00:57:12.580
It's used all the time.

00:57:12.580 --> 00:57:14.770
It's used for supply
chain, it's used

00:57:14.770 --> 00:57:19.670
for all sorts of other
crazy stuff all the time.

00:57:19.670 --> 00:57:23.660
It's used for scheduling
a lot of stuff.

00:57:23.660 --> 00:57:27.770
Now it gets kind of
interesting because you'd

00:57:27.770 --> 00:57:30.410
think to detect a linear
program is kind of easy.

00:57:30.410 --> 00:57:32.510
But it turns out there's
a whole bunch of problems

00:57:32.510 --> 00:57:33.560
that you can transform.

00:57:33.560 --> 00:57:37.190
If you know a few tricks, you
will know them in a few weeks.

00:57:37.190 --> 00:57:38.570
If you know a few
tricks, you can

00:57:38.570 --> 00:57:42.860
transform a problem that doesn't
remotely sound like an LP

00:57:42.860 --> 00:57:44.840
into an LP.

00:57:44.840 --> 00:57:46.820
And by the way, and
the way we think

00:57:46.820 --> 00:57:49.910
of that, that's actually kind of
the way it works in this class.

00:57:49.910 --> 00:57:53.210
If we reduce your practical
problem to a convex problem,

00:57:53.210 --> 00:57:54.740
we declare success.

00:57:54.740 --> 00:57:56.768
We just say, done.

00:57:56.768 --> 00:57:58.560
And then if the person
comes back and says,

00:57:58.560 --> 00:58:00.435
yeah, but we need to do
it for gigantic ones,

00:58:00.435 --> 00:58:02.000
then we can help
out, making it work.

00:58:02.000 --> 00:58:03.530
Or they say, no, we have to
do it for hard real time,

00:58:03.530 --> 00:58:05.330
it has to be done in 20
milliseconds, blah, blah, blah,

00:58:05.330 --> 00:58:06.600
and you're like, OK, whatever.

00:58:06.600 --> 00:58:09.380
But the point is
bottom zeroth order,

00:58:09.380 --> 00:58:11.750
you reduce it to convex is done.

00:58:11.750 --> 00:58:13.340
And that actually kind of fits.

00:58:13.340 --> 00:58:15.410
I mean, I think that's
more than just a joke.

00:58:15.410 --> 00:58:18.470
It's actually kind of
real that every time,

00:58:18.470 --> 00:58:23.030
historically, when someone
formulate something as a convex

00:58:23.030 --> 00:58:25.730
problem and what people
wanted to scale it or make it

00:58:25.730 --> 00:58:27.770
unbelievably reliable
or something,

00:58:27.770 --> 00:58:29.390
they were able to do it.

00:58:29.390 --> 00:58:31.670
So that's an example.

00:58:31.670 --> 00:58:34.310
Yeah, so we'll look at that.

00:58:34.310 --> 00:58:37.530
And then that gets us to
what the class is about,

00:58:37.530 --> 00:58:38.780
which is convex optimization.

00:58:38.780 --> 00:58:42.440
And so it's all about--

00:58:42.440 --> 00:58:45.710
I mean, kind of weird that the
entire class is about this.

00:58:45.710 --> 00:58:48.080
So it's actually very weird.

00:58:48.080 --> 00:58:54.590
It's a mild generalization
of being well affine.

00:58:54.590 --> 00:58:57.500
And here, I'll try to
use the notation here.

00:58:57.500 --> 00:59:04.430
It basically says that
f of alpha x plus beta y

00:59:04.430 --> 00:59:09.650
is less than or equal to alpha
of f of x plus beta f of y.

00:59:09.650 --> 00:59:11.180
Now there are some rules here.

00:59:11.180 --> 00:59:15.710
Alpha beta have to be positive
and they have to sum to 1.

00:59:15.710 --> 00:59:18.740
So roughly speaking,
this means--

00:59:18.740 --> 00:59:23.180
and obviously, we'll go over
the rest of all this later.

00:59:23.180 --> 00:59:27.080
But in one dimension, basically
it says that the curvature,

00:59:27.080 --> 00:59:28.610
it has to curve up.

00:59:28.610 --> 00:59:29.510
That's all it means.

00:59:29.510 --> 00:59:33.500
It has positive curvature,
non-negative curvature.

00:59:33.500 --> 00:59:37.940
So the entire class is about
optimization with things that

00:59:37.940 --> 00:59:39.890
have non-negative curvature.

00:59:39.890 --> 00:59:41.630
That's the entire class.

00:59:41.630 --> 00:59:44.210
What's weird-- it is
weird when you first

00:59:44.210 --> 00:59:47.450
hear about this is that
there's some weird asymmetry.

00:59:47.450 --> 00:59:51.860
That is very weird because
the classical thinking

00:59:51.860 --> 00:59:55.670
is if everything is linear--

00:59:55.670 --> 00:59:56.820
that's linear programming.

00:59:56.820 --> 01:00:00.020
In fact, in classical
Western optimization,

01:00:00.020 --> 01:00:02.390
the way people think of it
is there's linear programming

01:00:02.390 --> 01:00:04.515
and then-- are you ready
for the other one-- that's

01:00:04.515 --> 01:00:07.010
called non-linear programming.

01:00:07.010 --> 01:00:09.593
And you're supposed to go, ooh,
wow, that's really complicated

01:00:09.593 --> 01:00:10.468
and blah, blah, blah.

01:00:10.468 --> 01:00:11.610
OK, everybody got it?

01:00:11.610 --> 01:00:15.620
So it turns out that view of the
world is as stupid and wrong.

01:00:15.620 --> 01:00:21.830
Then what's really weird is it's
this-- it turns out curvature,

01:00:21.830 --> 01:00:26.180
which is nonlinearity, turns
out this is weird asymmetry

01:00:26.180 --> 01:00:30.300
and non-negative
curvature is just fine.

01:00:30.300 --> 01:00:33.420
But negative curvature--
that does make it hard.

01:00:33.420 --> 01:00:36.710
So I don't think this is
obvious ahead of time.

01:00:36.710 --> 01:00:40.400
This is kind of an
interesting part about it.

01:00:40.400 --> 01:00:43.070
So that's a convex optimization
problem where all the functions

01:00:43.070 --> 01:00:45.200
look like that.

01:00:45.200 --> 01:00:47.720
And it's like
linear programming.

01:00:47.720 --> 01:00:50.510
There's analytical solutions
for about 17 of these.

01:00:50.510 --> 01:00:52.970
And really, who cares
about those anyway?

01:00:52.970 --> 01:00:55.790
So least squares
and I could name

01:00:55.790 --> 01:00:57.320
a couple of other famous ones.

01:00:57.320 --> 01:00:59.600
You encounter one in
your information theory

01:00:59.600 --> 01:01:01.100
or communications class.

01:01:01.100 --> 01:01:03.950
In statistics, you see a couple.

01:01:03.950 --> 01:01:07.580
But I mean, it's
whatever, it's fine.

01:01:07.580 --> 01:01:10.550
They're reliable and
efficient algorithms.

01:01:10.550 --> 01:01:14.120
And it's kind of
almost a technology.

01:01:14.120 --> 01:01:15.530
It's not quite.

01:01:15.530 --> 01:01:17.800
Oh, that reminds me to
say something about--

01:01:17.800 --> 01:01:21.600
well, I'll say it again
when we get to it later.

01:01:21.600 --> 01:01:23.870
Later in the class,
within three weeks,

01:01:23.870 --> 01:01:28.010
you'll actually be
solving problems.

01:01:28.010 --> 01:01:33.380
It will mostly work, the
software, 99.5% of the time,

01:01:33.380 --> 01:01:34.970
something like that.

01:01:34.970 --> 01:01:36.060
It will fail sometimes.

01:01:36.060 --> 01:01:38.060
But actually, you don't
know it, but by agreeing

01:01:38.060 --> 01:01:40.130
to be in this class--
again, if you scroll down

01:01:40.130 --> 01:01:42.440
that thing that
you agreed, which

01:01:42.440 --> 01:01:44.880
you can't find anymore because
we actually didn't do that.

01:01:44.880 --> 01:01:47.690
But anyway, if
you look in there,

01:01:47.690 --> 01:01:51.140
it basically says that while
you're in the class at least,

01:01:51.140 --> 01:01:53.850
you agree to do the following.

01:01:53.850 --> 01:01:57.080
If it doesn't work, you
may only discuss that when

01:01:57.080 --> 01:01:59.340
people in the class are present.

01:01:59.340 --> 01:02:02.305
If a roommate says,
what are you doing--

01:02:02.305 --> 01:02:03.930
let's say you're
swearing at the laptop

01:02:03.930 --> 01:02:05.250
because it's not working.

01:02:05.250 --> 01:02:06.720
They say, well,
I'm doing convex--

01:02:06.720 --> 01:02:08.220
and they say, is it not working?

01:02:08.220 --> 01:02:11.850
You are obligated to say,
it's convex optimization,

01:02:11.850 --> 01:02:13.530
of course it works perfectly.

01:02:13.530 --> 01:02:15.240
You say, hello,
excuse me, that's

01:02:15.240 --> 01:02:18.318
the point of the class,
that's the point of the field.

01:02:18.318 --> 01:02:19.110
Of course it works.

01:02:19.110 --> 01:02:21.840
And they go, then why are
you swearing at your laptop.

01:02:21.840 --> 01:02:25.530
And you go, oh, it's not
doing what I thought it would

01:02:25.530 --> 01:02:26.550
or something like that.

01:02:26.550 --> 01:02:28.440
So anyway, I'm just
saying that's the way

01:02:28.440 --> 01:02:30.030
it's going to work.

01:02:30.030 --> 01:02:34.020
But it should be a technology
and, actually, it can easily

01:02:34.020 --> 01:02:35.250
be made a technology.

01:02:35.250 --> 01:02:45.240
So it's embedded in all sorts
of stuff that it's in UAVs,

01:02:45.240 --> 01:02:48.300
it's in lots and
lots of stuff where--

01:02:48.300 --> 01:02:50.460
and there is no
human intervention.

01:02:50.460 --> 01:02:53.770
It's running things like 100
hertz or something like that.

01:02:53.770 --> 01:02:55.030
So things work that way.

01:02:57.720 --> 01:03:01.160
Now here's where it
gets interesting--

01:03:01.160 --> 01:03:04.970
so the question then
comes down to, OK, cool,

01:03:04.970 --> 01:03:07.480
how do I know if my
problem is convex.

01:03:07.480 --> 01:03:09.040
That's a great question.

01:03:09.040 --> 01:03:13.760
And it turns out that's
actually not easy to do.

01:03:13.760 --> 01:03:16.983
That's actually
interesting that it's not--

01:03:16.983 --> 01:03:19.150
how do I know if my problem
is in a linear program--

01:03:19.150 --> 01:03:21.400
that's actually quite
straightforward.

01:03:21.400 --> 01:03:23.170
How do I know if it's--

01:03:23.170 --> 01:03:25.060
and you'll see
there'll be-- so that's

01:03:25.060 --> 01:03:28.600
why we're going to spend
the next three weeks going

01:03:28.600 --> 01:03:30.400
into some horrible math.

01:03:30.400 --> 01:03:33.040
But the whole point
is that you learn

01:03:33.040 --> 01:03:36.640
to recognize convex functions
and that sort of stuff.

01:03:36.640 --> 01:03:39.800
That's going to be the key.

01:03:39.800 --> 01:03:41.990
And it turns out
a lot of problems

01:03:41.990 --> 01:03:43.880
can be solved using this.

01:03:46.820 --> 01:03:51.590
So let's look at a quick
example just for fun here.

01:03:51.590 --> 01:03:56.090
Quasi practical actually, it
does come from something real.

01:03:56.090 --> 01:03:58.920
So here's the problem is--

01:03:58.920 --> 01:04:05.030
so I have a bunch of lamps here
that spray out illumination.

01:04:05.030 --> 01:04:08.390
And there's some surface that
I want to illuminate here.

01:04:08.390 --> 01:04:13.410
And I'm going to assume
that the light is incoherent

01:04:13.410 --> 01:04:13.910
or whatever.

01:04:13.910 --> 01:04:15.890
So the powers are going to add.

01:04:15.890 --> 01:04:17.570
They're going to add linearly.

01:04:17.570 --> 01:04:20.900
And it turns out that the
intensity-- here's a patch

01:04:20.900 --> 01:04:27.260
and the intensity here is simply
is linear in the lamp powers.

01:04:27.260 --> 01:04:31.880
And so akj gives
you the number--

01:04:31.880 --> 01:04:35.480
it's the coefficient of
the amount of illumination

01:04:35.480 --> 01:04:43.640
you get on patch k for, let's
say, 1 watt put into lamp j.

01:04:43.640 --> 01:04:47.400
And look, here's a baby
formula inverse square thing

01:04:47.400 --> 01:04:49.680
because it's propagating in 3D.

01:04:49.680 --> 01:04:52.170
And the cosine-- this is like
the cosine shading angle.

01:04:52.170 --> 01:04:53.050
It doesn't matter.

01:04:53.050 --> 01:04:56.220
And if your patch is here
and the light is here,

01:04:56.220 --> 01:04:57.540
you don't have a direct thing.

01:04:57.540 --> 01:04:59.670
So it's 0.

01:04:59.670 --> 01:05:02.910
OK, so that's what it is.

01:05:02.910 --> 01:05:07.890
And the question here is to
choose these lamp powers--

01:05:07.890 --> 01:05:09.780
they've obviously got
to be non-negative.

01:05:09.780 --> 01:05:12.450
This problem would
be a lot easier

01:05:12.450 --> 01:05:16.080
if you had lamps that
could spew negative light.

01:05:16.080 --> 01:05:20.190
That would be a huge innovation.

01:05:20.190 --> 01:05:24.690
So spray darkness, I guess.

01:05:24.690 --> 01:05:26.340
And they have some
maximum value.

01:05:26.340 --> 01:05:29.280
And what you want
is someone who said,

01:05:29.280 --> 01:05:32.430
I want uniform illumination at
this level called I desired.

01:05:32.430 --> 01:05:33.970
I care about proportional.

01:05:33.970 --> 01:05:35.370
So I'm going to take logs.

01:05:35.370 --> 01:05:41.130
And I'd say minimize the maximum
fractional error across this.

01:05:41.130 --> 01:05:42.930
So that's the problem.

01:05:42.930 --> 01:05:44.988
So this is a typical
real problem.

01:05:44.988 --> 01:05:46.530
It actually came
from something real.

01:05:46.530 --> 01:05:47.850
It's fine, that's it.

01:05:47.850 --> 01:05:49.210
That's the problem.

01:05:49.210 --> 01:05:51.390
So the question is,
how do you do this.

01:05:51.390 --> 01:05:53.200
And this wouldn't be a problem.

01:05:53.200 --> 01:05:56.760
I mean, engineers are
actually pretty good.

01:05:56.760 --> 01:05:59.430
The first thing someone
would do, I would hope,

01:05:59.430 --> 01:06:02.260
would be to do
things like this--

01:06:02.260 --> 01:06:04.260
first of all, they
have a simulator.

01:06:04.260 --> 01:06:05.640
And the first
thing they would do

01:06:05.640 --> 01:06:07.350
is they'd fire up the simulator.

01:06:07.350 --> 01:06:10.140
And you should just
put in uniform power

01:06:10.140 --> 01:06:13.620
and then turn it up and down
and see how well you do.

01:06:13.620 --> 01:06:15.820
Then they'd say, oh,
I know least squares.

01:06:15.820 --> 01:06:18.368
So I'm going to do least
squares because this

01:06:18.368 --> 01:06:19.410
is kind of least squares.

01:06:19.410 --> 01:06:23.160
There's a matrix there
that maps the powers

01:06:23.160 --> 01:06:24.390
to the illuminations.

01:06:24.390 --> 01:06:27.450
So I'm going to
do least squares.

01:06:27.450 --> 01:06:30.720
Now what's going
to happen when you

01:06:30.720 --> 01:06:33.450
do least squares is
some of the powers

01:06:33.450 --> 01:06:34.830
are going to come out negative.

01:06:34.830 --> 01:06:36.210
I mean, duh.

01:06:36.210 --> 01:06:39.120
And then this is
street fighting,

01:06:39.120 --> 01:06:40.890
this is someone who
just has to do this.

01:06:40.890 --> 01:06:43.350
They're like, I just need
to find these illuminations.

01:06:43.350 --> 01:06:46.600
If it's negative, you just set
that lamp to 0 or something.

01:06:46.600 --> 01:06:49.590
And if it's above
the maximum power,

01:06:49.590 --> 01:06:51.630
you clip it at
the maximum power.

01:06:51.630 --> 01:06:54.750
And so something like this,
it would do something.

01:06:54.750 --> 01:06:55.830
I don't know what.

01:06:55.830 --> 01:06:57.703
Probably not terrible.

01:06:57.703 --> 01:07:00.120
And then you'd say, well, no,
I know what I'm going to do,

01:07:00.120 --> 01:07:01.745
I'm going to do
weighted least squares.

01:07:01.745 --> 01:07:03.870
And the way I'm
going to do it is I'm

01:07:03.870 --> 01:07:05.910
going to have this least
squares objective here.

01:07:05.910 --> 01:07:07.950
But that's another
least squares objective.

01:07:07.950 --> 01:07:10.200
And what it does, if
you think about it,

01:07:10.200 --> 01:07:12.810
is there's the interval each
of those powers has to be in,

01:07:12.810 --> 01:07:14.700
which is between 0 and p max.

01:07:14.700 --> 01:07:19.800
And what this is it pulls
you towards the center

01:07:19.800 --> 01:07:20.530
of that interval.

01:07:20.530 --> 01:07:22.447
So if you're too low
like your negative power,

01:07:22.447 --> 01:07:23.910
it pulls you towards zero.

01:07:23.910 --> 01:07:27.097
If you're positive, if
you're above the maximum,

01:07:27.097 --> 01:07:28.680
it pulls you down
towards the maximum.

01:07:28.680 --> 01:07:30.010
Everybody got that?

01:07:30.010 --> 01:07:34.000
And so you might
solve this problem.

01:07:34.000 --> 01:07:36.660
And then here would be a
completely reasonable way

01:07:36.660 --> 01:07:42.230
to do this-- if p is
below negative increase,

01:07:42.230 --> 01:07:46.162
you say wj times equals 2.

01:07:46.162 --> 01:07:47.370
This is what people would do.

01:07:47.370 --> 01:07:49.203
This is the kind of
thing somebody would do.

01:07:49.203 --> 01:07:53.970
If it's above the
maximum, w times equals 2.

01:07:53.970 --> 01:07:56.388
If you're in the band,
w divided by equals 2.

01:07:56.388 --> 01:07:57.930
Everybody understand
what I'm saying?

01:07:57.930 --> 01:07:59.880
OK, so you just do that.

01:07:59.880 --> 01:08:01.510
And something is
going to happen.

01:08:01.510 --> 01:08:03.360
It's not going to be too bad.

01:08:03.360 --> 01:08:06.990
And that's how a lot of stuff
actually works this way.

01:08:06.990 --> 01:08:09.480
As I told you,
you could actually

01:08:09.480 --> 01:08:12.020
have a perfectly good life doing
a lot of mathematical stuff

01:08:12.020 --> 01:08:13.770
if the only thing you
actually really knew

01:08:13.770 --> 01:08:16.170
well was least squares plus
you knew some street fighting

01:08:16.170 --> 01:08:16.890
tricks.

01:08:16.890 --> 01:08:17.835
So you'd be just fine.

01:08:20.460 --> 01:08:22.080
Somebody could say--
maybe they came

01:08:22.080 --> 01:08:25.890
from management science
or OR and they go, hey,

01:08:25.890 --> 01:08:29.880
that looks just like an LP
or something I can solve

01:08:29.880 --> 01:08:32.382
with LP except for the log.

01:08:32.382 --> 01:08:34.090
And so they might do
something like this.

01:08:34.090 --> 01:08:35.560
And you'd do pretty well.

01:08:35.560 --> 01:08:37.649
And these are just
approximate solutions.

01:08:37.649 --> 01:08:38.910
But probably not too bad.

01:08:38.910 --> 01:08:40.439
Everybody following this?

01:08:40.439 --> 01:08:43.710
I mention this because this
is kind of how a lot of stuff

01:08:43.710 --> 01:08:44.580
actually works.

01:08:44.580 --> 01:08:45.930
People are good.

01:08:45.930 --> 01:08:49.600
They'll do what they
need and clever.

01:08:49.600 --> 01:08:52.359
So it turns out the whole
thing is just convex,

01:08:52.359 --> 01:08:54.790
period, the whole thing.

01:08:54.790 --> 01:08:57.910
I mean, you exponentiate the
objective and this is convex.

01:08:57.910 --> 01:09:00.939
And it's not obvious, and
people probably don't know that.

01:09:00.939 --> 01:09:05.800
So this is the function that
is the fractional error.

01:09:05.800 --> 01:09:07.750
And let me explain that.

01:09:07.750 --> 01:09:13.840
So u is the ratio of
two positive things.

01:09:13.840 --> 01:09:16.240
If it's one, they're equal
and that's the minimum.

01:09:16.240 --> 01:09:19.420
You could subtract 1 if you
want here and make this 0.

01:09:19.420 --> 01:09:21.330
But if you increase
it above that,

01:09:21.330 --> 01:09:23.080
then your fractional
error grows linearly.

01:09:23.080 --> 01:09:25.600
If you decrease it,
it's like 1 over u.

01:09:25.600 --> 01:09:28.910
So I think you could call
this 100% error or something.

01:09:28.910 --> 01:09:32.680
And if I were down here, the
50% error points would be 1/2

01:09:32.680 --> 01:09:35.950
and, I don't know, 1.5?

01:09:35.950 --> 01:09:38.710
No, somehow, that
didn't come out right.

01:09:38.710 --> 01:09:39.729
Scrap that.

01:09:39.729 --> 01:09:41.800
But anyway, you get the idea.

01:09:41.800 --> 01:09:43.643
But the main thing
here and the only thing

01:09:43.643 --> 01:09:46.060
you should be able to do now
is take your eyeball and say,

01:09:46.060 --> 01:09:50.270
that curves upward,
that's convex.

01:09:50.270 --> 01:09:52.850
So make sense?

01:09:52.850 --> 01:09:55.440
So that's the idea.

01:09:55.440 --> 01:09:58.470
So you get the exact
solution and I mean,

01:09:58.470 --> 01:10:01.730
it's just not a problem.

01:10:01.730 --> 01:10:06.440
And now for fun, I'll add
some constraints to it,

01:10:06.440 --> 01:10:09.150
and we'll check what happens.

01:10:09.150 --> 01:10:12.330
So two proposed constraints.

01:10:12.330 --> 01:10:15.360
The first is that no more
than half the total power

01:10:15.360 --> 01:10:17.310
is in any 10 lamps.

01:10:17.310 --> 01:10:20.460
I could probably make up
some kind of practical reason

01:10:20.460 --> 01:10:22.463
why you might impose a
constraint like that.

01:10:22.463 --> 01:10:23.880
But usually,
constraints like that

01:10:23.880 --> 01:10:24.870
are just made up
by people who have

01:10:24.870 --> 01:10:26.080
no idea what they're doing.

01:10:26.080 --> 01:10:27.247
And so they just made it up.

01:10:30.430 --> 01:10:35.898
The second is this-- no more
than half the lamps are on.

01:10:35.898 --> 01:10:38.190
By the way, that gets us to
something very interesting,

01:10:38.190 --> 01:10:41.150
which is actually
suppose you were

01:10:41.150 --> 01:10:43.040
designing like a building
or a lab experiment

01:10:43.040 --> 01:10:44.832
or some-- who knows--
a piece of equipment,

01:10:44.832 --> 01:10:48.380
and you wanted to choose
where to put the lights.

01:10:48.380 --> 01:10:50.810
So you could very
reasonably do something

01:10:50.810 --> 01:10:53.990
like solve this problem with
a million potential light

01:10:53.990 --> 01:11:02.100
locations, but you'd like to
solve it with 990 or whatever,

01:11:02.100 --> 01:11:04.830
with 99% of them 0.

01:11:04.830 --> 01:11:09.090
Because if a lamp is 0, it
means you didn't need it.

01:11:09.090 --> 01:11:11.130
And in fact, people design
structures that way.

01:11:11.130 --> 01:11:13.890
You take a big
old structure, you

01:11:13.890 --> 01:11:16.200
put in way too many
bars or a space frame,

01:11:16.200 --> 01:11:18.390
you put way too many bars
in you're going to need,

01:11:18.390 --> 01:11:21.870
and your variables are
the cross sectional areas.

01:11:21.870 --> 01:11:25.080
And what you're
hoping, then you'd say,

01:11:25.080 --> 01:11:27.750
it's got to support
my loads, it has

01:11:27.750 --> 01:11:30.660
to satisfy these dynamics,
blah, blah, blah.

01:11:30.660 --> 01:11:32.130
And what you're
hoping-- you have

01:11:32.130 --> 01:11:36.000
no intention of building a
bridge with 100 million bars.

01:11:36.000 --> 01:11:38.400
And then if you're
lucky, it comes out

01:11:38.400 --> 01:11:41.520
and the solution is sparse.

01:11:41.520 --> 01:11:44.610
and congratulations, you
just designed the topology

01:11:44.610 --> 01:11:45.365
of the Truss.

01:11:45.365 --> 01:11:47.490
I don't know if that made
sense, but that was fine.

01:11:47.490 --> 01:11:48.323
Architects love you.

01:11:48.323 --> 01:11:48.840
What?

01:11:48.840 --> 01:11:50.190
Architects love you too.

01:11:50.190 --> 01:11:52.530
[INAUDIBLE] using material.

01:11:52.530 --> 01:11:53.070
Yeah.

01:11:53.070 --> 01:11:55.200
No, no, exactly.

01:11:55.200 --> 01:12:00.490
All right, so I
mean, it's here--

01:12:00.490 --> 01:12:03.360
so it turns out these
two look very similar

01:12:03.360 --> 01:12:07.560
and they both look kind of
weird and combinatorial.

01:12:07.560 --> 01:12:10.480
I mean, later in the class,
you might say, I don't know,

01:12:10.480 --> 01:12:12.390
they both look kind of hard.

01:12:12.390 --> 01:12:18.170
So it turns out
this one is convex.

01:12:18.170 --> 01:12:20.940
It's very weird, but it is.

01:12:20.940 --> 01:12:23.000
And the second one is not.

01:12:23.000 --> 01:12:23.960
I could repeat this.

01:12:23.960 --> 01:12:27.380
I could transcribe this
to any field you like.

01:12:27.380 --> 01:12:33.350
It would be construct me a
portfolio of 10,000 assets

01:12:33.350 --> 01:12:34.680
world market or whatever.

01:12:34.680 --> 01:12:36.470
But I don't want
to have more than--

01:12:36.470 --> 01:12:38.690
I don't want to hold
more than 100 names.

01:12:38.690 --> 01:12:41.120
That's an example of that.

01:12:41.120 --> 01:12:42.410
That's actually hard.

01:12:42.410 --> 01:12:45.740
But I could put a
concentration in--

01:12:45.740 --> 01:12:47.240
I could say things like this--

01:12:47.240 --> 01:12:50.630
I don't want more than
5% of my portfolio

01:12:50.630 --> 01:12:53.600
to live in any
collection of 100 names.

01:12:53.600 --> 01:12:54.710
Everybody follow that?

01:12:54.710 --> 01:12:55.550
Someone says, why
would you do that.

01:12:55.550 --> 01:12:57.980
And you go, that's an anti
concentration blah, blah, blah,

01:12:57.980 --> 01:12:59.140
inequality, constraint.

01:12:59.140 --> 01:13:00.650
Anyway, that one's convex.

01:13:00.650 --> 01:13:02.720
This is convex.

01:13:02.720 --> 01:13:05.312
This is weird, but
it actually tells you

01:13:05.312 --> 01:13:06.270
a very important point.

01:13:06.270 --> 01:13:07.853
It's actually why
you're here and it's

01:13:07.853 --> 01:13:12.260
why the class is
not two lectures.

01:13:12.260 --> 01:13:16.020
So I like to think
of it this way--

01:13:16.020 --> 01:13:18.020
in fact, that's part of
what the class is about.

01:13:18.020 --> 01:13:22.160
We are going to break your
idea about what's easy

01:13:22.160 --> 01:13:25.280
and what's hard for an
optimization problem

01:13:25.280 --> 01:13:27.110
because unless you
know the things you're

01:13:27.110 --> 01:13:29.390
going to know in this
class, you are wrong

01:13:29.390 --> 01:13:32.630
and it is not obvious.

01:13:32.630 --> 01:13:34.310
Which is kind of interesting.

01:13:34.310 --> 01:13:37.130
If I show you a linear program
and I tell you, oh, my God,

01:13:37.130 --> 01:13:38.510
it's got this
dimensions, there's

01:13:38.510 --> 01:13:40.913
more vertices than the
number of subatomic particles

01:13:40.913 --> 01:13:41.580
in the universe.

01:13:41.580 --> 01:13:44.173
And you're like, whoa,
whoa, whoa, whoa, whoa.

01:13:44.173 --> 01:13:45.840
And then I'd say,
yeah, I can solve that

01:13:45.840 --> 01:13:48.510
in 50 milliseconds on my phone.

01:13:48.510 --> 01:13:54.090
So I'm just saying, these things
are not obvious, they're weird.

01:13:54.090 --> 01:13:57.150
Or another way I would usually
say something like this

01:13:57.150 --> 01:14:02.820
that this is I guess
problem complexity is not

01:14:02.820 --> 01:14:05.670
continuous in the
intuition norm.

01:14:05.670 --> 01:14:07.020
That's the way to say it.

01:14:07.020 --> 01:14:11.220
So it means two things can look
intuitively to be very similar.

01:14:11.220 --> 01:14:13.470
You're like, yeah, whatever,
they look about the same.

01:14:13.470 --> 01:14:15.630
But one can be really hard
and one is really easy.

01:14:15.630 --> 01:14:17.940
So that's the idea.

01:14:22.360 --> 01:14:24.010
Let's see.

01:14:24.010 --> 01:14:26.667
So this is this example.

01:14:26.667 --> 01:14:28.750
I'll just say a little bit
about the course goals,

01:14:28.750 --> 01:14:32.380
and then I think we'll
just finish up today.

01:14:32.380 --> 01:14:38.650
So the main goal is to be able
to recognize convex problems.

01:14:38.650 --> 01:14:41.723
And so the reason
is it is not going

01:14:41.723 --> 01:14:44.140
to happen to you that you go
to an internship or a startup

01:14:44.140 --> 01:14:46.140
or something and
someone says, whoa, I

01:14:46.140 --> 01:14:47.515
have this optimization
problem, I

01:14:47.515 --> 01:14:48.890
think it might be
kind of convex.

01:14:48.890 --> 01:14:50.020
It's not going to happen.

01:14:50.020 --> 01:14:51.370
What's going to happen is
they're going to come to you

01:14:51.370 --> 01:14:53.770
and it's going to be
unbelievably complicated.

01:14:53.770 --> 01:14:56.620
And they're going to say, oh,
do you know about mortgage swap

01:14:56.620 --> 01:14:58.090
derivative options.

01:14:58.090 --> 01:14:59.350
And you're like, oh.

01:14:59.350 --> 01:15:01.880
And then they'd say, well,
here's the basics of this

01:15:01.880 --> 01:15:03.713
and here's that and
here's that and oh yeah,

01:15:03.713 --> 01:15:06.773
here's a giant book, read that,
and an intern fit this model.

01:15:06.773 --> 01:15:07.690
And you're like, whoa.

01:15:07.690 --> 01:15:10.330
So it's going to be
really complicated.

01:15:10.330 --> 01:15:12.522
This is the way it really works.

01:15:12.522 --> 01:15:14.980
And it's going to be your job
to cut through all the noise,

01:15:14.980 --> 01:15:20.240
sit back, come back the
next day and go, it's done.

01:15:20.240 --> 01:15:23.120
I can schedule your EV charging.

01:15:23.120 --> 01:15:26.188
And they'll go, what do you
mean, it's really complicated,

01:15:26.188 --> 01:15:27.980
we have limits on the
total amount of power

01:15:27.980 --> 01:15:30.140
here and blah, blah, blah,
and different customers

01:15:30.140 --> 01:15:32.990
have different goals about
when they want so much charge.

01:15:32.990 --> 01:15:34.970
And you're like, it's done.

01:15:34.970 --> 01:15:36.350
You'll just say it's done.

01:15:36.350 --> 01:15:37.940
So that's what we're
training you for.

01:15:40.910 --> 01:15:43.100
Weirdly, that actually happens.

01:15:43.100 --> 01:15:48.080
I've actually had students
come back to me the fall,

01:15:48.080 --> 01:15:48.950
the next year.

01:15:48.950 --> 01:15:50.880
One came back and I
was like, what is it.

01:15:50.880 --> 01:15:53.187
And he said, you said,
yeah, you rant and rave.

01:15:53.187 --> 01:15:55.520
They said, we just thought
you were just telling stories

01:15:55.520 --> 01:15:56.603
and being weird and stuff.

01:15:56.603 --> 01:15:58.320
I was like, yeah, mostly, I am.

01:15:58.320 --> 01:16:00.620
And he said, yeah, but
what you said actually

01:16:00.620 --> 01:16:01.700
really happened to me.

01:16:01.700 --> 01:16:03.350
I actually really
went, and they said,

01:16:03.350 --> 01:16:04.780
oh, we want to make
this regression model,

01:16:04.780 --> 01:16:06.590
but it's super complicated
and there's this and that

01:16:06.590 --> 01:16:09.200
and you have to do this and
that and there's causality.

01:16:09.200 --> 01:16:12.170
And you can't use
this before that.

01:16:12.170 --> 01:16:15.110
And let's remember that the
Hong Kong thing, those prices

01:16:15.110 --> 01:16:16.383
closed before this opens.

01:16:16.383 --> 01:16:17.300
And you're like, whoa.

01:16:17.300 --> 01:16:21.148
Anyway, turned out there's
this convex, the whole thing.

01:16:21.148 --> 01:16:22.690
And I said, well,
what happened then.

01:16:22.690 --> 01:16:26.220
And he said, I was
the superstar intern.

01:16:26.220 --> 01:16:28.990
And he said, actually, it
was finished in about a week.

01:16:28.990 --> 01:16:31.740
But anyway, so it actually does
happen, just to let you know.

01:16:34.450 --> 01:16:37.180
And then it's very much,
the class, the focus

01:16:37.180 --> 01:16:39.340
is in no way whatsoever
on the theory.

01:16:39.340 --> 01:16:41.710
There's extremely
interesting theory here.

01:16:41.710 --> 01:16:46.990
But no, we're not
interested in that.

01:16:46.990 --> 01:16:49.670
We're actually interested
in solving problems.

01:16:49.670 --> 01:16:54.130
And so you will have practice
writing these 10 line scripts.

01:16:54.130 --> 01:16:55.378
You can't get out of here.

01:16:55.378 --> 01:16:56.920
You will have done
signal processing,

01:16:56.920 --> 01:16:59.770
you'll do machine learning,
you're going to do Control,

01:16:59.770 --> 01:17:03.730
you're going to do
portfolio construction,

01:17:03.730 --> 01:17:06.880
you're going to find bounds
on arbitrage free, bounds

01:17:06.880 --> 01:17:08.818
on prices.

01:17:08.818 --> 01:17:10.360
I don't know what
you're going to do,

01:17:10.360 --> 01:17:12.820
but you're going to do a lot
of weird practical stuff.

01:17:12.820 --> 01:17:15.020
I mean, kind of sandbox
versions of things.

01:17:15.020 --> 01:17:17.890
But still, they will
be completely real.

01:17:17.890 --> 01:17:22.570
So that's going to be the
focus of the class and that's

01:17:22.570 --> 01:17:25.930
kind of what we're
trying to produce.

01:17:25.930 --> 01:17:28.125
So maybe at the
end of the class,

01:17:28.125 --> 01:17:29.750
we're going to make
it all very simple,

01:17:29.750 --> 01:17:32.020
it's going to be kind of a--

01:17:32.020 --> 01:17:33.590
you're going to be in a sandbox.

01:17:33.590 --> 01:17:35.180
We'll predigest stuff.

01:17:35.180 --> 01:17:36.390
It'll be fine.

01:17:36.390 --> 01:17:38.300
But then maybe at
the end of the class,

01:17:38.300 --> 01:17:40.610
once you've mastered
all of those things,

01:17:40.610 --> 01:17:46.100
then I'll tell you maybe some
secret street fighting tricks

01:17:46.100 --> 01:17:48.360
before you go out into the wild.

01:17:48.360 --> 01:17:50.090
But I'll make you
sign a release saying,

01:17:50.090 --> 01:17:52.820
you didn't learn
those things from me.

01:17:52.820 --> 01:17:56.630
So we'll do that a bit later.

01:17:56.630 --> 01:18:02.240
So that's really the goal of the
class is to learn these things.

01:18:02.240 --> 01:18:05.990
So I'm going to quit here, but
I am going to say one thing--

01:18:05.990 --> 01:18:08.570
bear in mind that when
we come back Thursday,

01:18:08.570 --> 01:18:10.667
the tone is going to
change completely.

01:18:10.667 --> 01:18:12.500
You might be thinking,
wow, this is actually

01:18:12.500 --> 01:18:14.708
kind of interesting, this
is going to be a fun class.

01:18:14.708 --> 01:18:16.010
Wait till Thursday.

01:18:16.010 --> 01:18:18.470
We're going in way
deep and it is not--

01:18:18.470 --> 01:18:19.520
well, it'll be weird.

01:18:19.520 --> 01:18:22.600
But anyway, you'll see.

